scope`, then `flopscope-server`, then\n`flopscope-client`"},"383":{"id":"/docs/development/release-53","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Owner: `AIcrowd`"},"384":{"id":"/docs/development/release-54","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Repository name: `flopscope`"},"385":{"id":"/docs/development/release-55","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Workflow name: `pypi-publish.yml`"},"386":{"id":"/docs/development/release-56","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Environment name: `pypi`"},"387":{"id":"/docs/development/release-57","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Save each one."},"388":{"id":"/docs/development/release-58","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"**On GitHub — create the `pypi` environment with required reviewers\n(do this once; it is shared across all matrix-publish jobs):**"},"389":{"id":"/docs/development/release-59","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Go to https\\://github.com/AIcrowd/flopscope/settings/environments"},"390":{"id":"/docs/development/release-60","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Click \"New environment\", name it `pypi`"},"391":{"id":"/docs/development/release-61","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Under \"Deployment protection rules\" → \"Required reviewers\", add the\nrelease maintainers"},"392":{"id":"/docs/development/release-62","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"Save"},"393":{"id":"/docs/development/release-63","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#first-time-pypi-setup-one-time-per-project","content":"After all configured Trusted Publishers and the shared environment are\nset up, the next `v*` tag push will trigger the publish flow\nend-to-end — and a single approval click covers all matrix publishes."},"394":{"id":"/docs/development/release-64","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#manual-publish-from-a-maintainers-machine-fallback","content":"The normal path is the matrix workflow described above. Use this section only\nwhen GitHub Actions is unavailable or won't fire (Actions outage, repo policy\nglitch, etc.) and you need to ship a release right now. This path bypasses\nTrusted Publishing, the `pypi` environment approval gate, and the auto-created\nGitHub Release."},"395":{"id":"/docs/development/release-65","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"Install `twine` as a uv-managed tool\n(keeps it out of project dependencies):"},"396":{"id":"/docs/development/release-66","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"Generate three **project-scoped** PyPI API tokens, one per project. For\neach of `flopscope`, `flopscope-server`, `flopscope-client`:"},"397":{"id":"/docs/development/release-67","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"Go to https\\://pypi.org/manage/account/token/"},"398":{"id":"/docs/development/release-68","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"Click \"Add API token\""},"399":{"id":"/docs/development/release-69","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"**Token name:** something self-descriptive (e.g. `flopscope-manual-2026`)"},"400":{"id":"/docs/development/release-70","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"**Scope:** \"Project: flopscope\" (scope to a single project — not \"Entire account\")"},"401":{"id":"/docs/development/release-71","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"Copy the `pypi-...` token immediately; PyPI will not show it again"},"402":{"id":"/docs/development/release-72","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#one-time-setup","content":"Add all three tokens to `~/.pypirc` (chmod 600 the file). Per-project\nsections let twine pick the right one based on the wheel's distribution\nname:"},"403":{"id":"/docs/development/release-73","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#publishing-vxyz-manually","content":"Assumes the bump commit + `vX.Y.Z` tag already exist on `main` (use\n`cz bump --increment <PATCH|MINOR>` as usual to produce them; the\ndifference is only that the GH workflow won't run)."},"404":{"id":"/docs/development/release-74","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#publishing-vxyz-manually","content":"Build all three packages from the tagged commit:"},"405":{"id":"/docs/development/release-75","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#publishing-vxyz-manually","content":"Verify each wheel's metadata is what you expect — particularly the\n`Requires-Dist` cross-pin in flopscope-server and the `Provides-Extra: server`\nin flopscope:"},"406":{"id":"/docs/development/release-76","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#publishing-vxyz-manually","content":"Upload each wheel + sdist with `twine`, pointing at the per-project section\nof `~/.pypirc`:"},"407":{"id":"/docs/development/release-77","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#publishing-vxyz-manually","content":"Twine prints a https\\://pypi.org/project/.../ URL after each successful\nupload. If a previous attempt already uploaded any of the files, add\n`--skip-existing` to make the command idempotent."},"408":{"id":"/docs/development/release-78","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#publishing-vxyz-manually","content":"Create the GitHub Release manually (the workflow would normally do this):"},"409":{"id":"/docs/development/release-79","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#trade-offs-vs-the-workflow","content":"The manual path skips four safety nets the workflow gives you:"},"410":{"id":"/docs/development/release-80","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#trade-offs-vs-the-workflow","content":"**Environment approval gate.** Anyone with the API tokens can publish; there\nis no second-pair-of-eyes click. Scope tokens narrowly and rotate them after\nuse if leaked or no longer needed."},"411":{"id":"/docs/development/release-81","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#trade-offs-vs-the-workflow","content":"**Trusted Publishing audit trail.** PyPI records \"uploaded by API token X\"\ninstead of \"uploaded by GH workflow run #...\" — harder to trace."},"412":{"id":"/docs/development/release-82","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#trade-offs-vs-the-workflow","content":"**Auto-generated GitHub Release with notes from CHANGELOG.** Step 4 above\nis the manual replacement."},"413":{"id":"/docs/development/release-83","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#trade-offs-vs-the-workflow","content":"**Matrix isolation.** Network/IO failures during upload could leave a\npartial publish (e.g., flopscope uploaded but flopscope-server failed).\nIn that case re-run only the failed `twine upload` with `--skip-existing`."},"414":{"id":"/docs/development/release-84","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#trade-offs-vs-the-workflow","content":"Once Actions is healthy again, **switch back to the workflow path** — delete\nthe API tokens you created for the manual publish, and the next `vX.Y.Z` tag\npush will go through Trusted Publishing as normal."},"415":{"id":"/docs/development/release-85","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#related-references","content":"`[tool.commitizen]` in pyproject.toml — release tooling config"},"416":{"id":"/docs/development/release-86","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#related-references","content":"`.github/workflows/pypi-publish.yml` — the publish workflow"},"417":{"id":"/docs/development/release-87","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#related-references","content":"`scripts/check_version_sync.py` — version-sync checker"},"418":{"id":"/docs/development/release-88","page_id":"/docs/development/release","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/development/release#related-references","content":"Contributor Guide — repo layout, dev setup, commit conventions, `commit-msg` hook"},"419":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"page","content":"Competition Guide","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Getting Started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition"},"420":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-0","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#setting-a-flop-budget","content":"Setting a FLOP budget"},"421":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-1","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#decorator-form","content":"Decorator form"},"422":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-2","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"Wall-time limits"},"423":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-3","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"Reading the budget summary"},"424":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-4","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#quick-tips-for-competition","content":"Quick tips for competition"},"425":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-5","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#local-vs-remote-backends","content":"Local vs. remote backends"},"426":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-6","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#immutable-arrays","content":"Immutable arrays"},"427":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-7","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#when-things-go-wrong","content":"When things go wrong"},"428":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-8","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"*Everything you need to compete within a FLOP budget.*"},"429":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-9","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"**You will learn:**"},"430":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-10","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"How to set budget limits with `BudgetContext`"},"431":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-11","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"How to use the `@flops.budget` decorator form"},"432":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-12","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"How wall-time limits work via `wall_time_limit_s`"},"433":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-13","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"How to read budget summaries"},"434":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-14","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition","content":"Common competition pitfalls and tips"},"435":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-15","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#setting-a-flop-budget","content":"Every competition submission runs inside a FLOP budget. Use `BudgetContext` to declare how many FLOPs your code is allowed to spend:"},"436":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-16","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#setting-a-flop-budget","content":"If your code exceeds the budget, Flopscope raises `BudgetExhaustedError` *before* the offending operation executes. The error message includes the cost of the failed operation and the remaining budget."},"437":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-17","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#setting-a-flop-budget","content":"The `namespace` parameter sets the root namespace prefix for that budget context. Nested `fnp.namespace(...)` scopes extend it with dotted segments, but they do not create child budgets or split the FLOP limit into separate pools."},"438":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-18","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#decorator-form","content":"For cleaner code, use `@flops.budget` to attach a budget directly to a function:"},"439":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-19","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#decorator-form","content":"Each call to the decorated function runs inside the same `BudgetContext`; the namespace is only the root prefix used for attribution, not a separate budget pool. Repeated calls reuse that context and keep accumulating on the same budget and operation log."},"440":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-20","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"In addition to FLOP budgets, competitions may enforce a wall-clock time limit via `wall_time_limit_s`. This prevents solutions from stalling on operations that are analytically cheap but slow in practice:"},"441":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-21","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"If the wall-clock time is exceeded, Flopscope raises `TimeExhaustedError`. The timer starts when the context is entered and is checked after each counted operation."},"442":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-22","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"What `wall_time_limit_s` does and does not do:"},"443":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-23","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"It is a `BudgetContext` setting, so you configure it in the same place you set `flop_budget`."},"444":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-24","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"It measures total wall-clock time for the active context, not FLOPs."},"445":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-25","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"It is checked cooperatively before and after counted NumPy calls, so overshoot is bounded by the duration of one NumPy call."},"446":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-26","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#wall-time-limits","content":"It is a clean diagnostic limit inside flopscope. Hard process/container kills still belong to the outer execution environment."},"447":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-27","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"Call `budget.summary()` when you want the current context's summary, or `flops.budget_summary()` for the accumulated session/global view. Both stay flat by default; use `by_namespace=True` only when you want a namespace breakdown:"},"448":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-28","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"Use these forms for different questions:"},"449":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-29","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"`budget.summary()` answers \"what did this one explicit context spend?\""},"450":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-30","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"`flops.budget_summary()` answers \"what has this process/session spent overall?\""},"451":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-31","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"`budget.summary_dict(...)` and `flops.budget_summary_dict(...)` return the same information as structured data instead of formatted text."},"452":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-32","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"The block below shows `print(budget.summary(by_namespace=True))` for the `solver` context:"},"453":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-33","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"Key things to look for:"},"454":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-34","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"**Budget / Used / Remaining:** the top rows show the explicit competition budget, current spend, and remaining headroom"},"455":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-35","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"**By namespace:** `solver` is the root prefix, and nested scopes show up as dotted paths like `solver.precompute`. Use `budget.summary(by_namespace=True)` for the current context or `flops.budget_summary(by_namespace=True)` for the accumulated session/global view"},"456":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-36","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"**By operation:** this toy pass is dominated by the single `einsum`; `exp` and `sum` are tiny by comparison"},"457":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-37","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"**Wall / backend / flopscope overhead / residual time:** wall time is total elapsed time for the context. Flopscope backend time is spent inside the underlying NumPy / BLAS / LAPACK calls being counted (including data-movement ops like `tile`, `take`, `pad`). Flopscope overhead is spent in flopscope's own dispatch code (wrapper preambles, FLOP cost computation, view-casts, post-call wrapping, `maybe_check_nan_inf` when opted in). Residual wall time is the measured remainder outside backend calls and flopscope overhead (user Python between ops, sleeps, GC pauses, and the callback time of ops like `apply_along_axis`). The decomposition is exact: `wall_time_s = flopscope_backend_time_s + flopscope_overhead_time_s + residual_wall_time_s`"},"458":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-38","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"**Flat default:** the default summary stays flat unless you opt into `by_namespace=True`"},"459":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-39","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#reading-the-budget-summary","content":"For programmatic access, use `flops.budget_summary_dict()`:"},"460":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-40","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#quick-tips-for-competition","content":"**Check costs before committing budget.** Use cost query functions to estimate before executing:"},"461":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-41","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#quick-tips-for-competition","content":"**Use namespaces for phases.** Split your solution into named phases (e.g., `\"init\"`, `\"solve\"`, `\"refine\"`) so the budget summary shows exactly where FLOPs are spent."},"462":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-42","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#quick-tips-for-competition","content":"**Exploit symmetry for savings.** If your tensors are symmetric, wrapping them with `flops.as_symmetric()` can halve pointwise costs and significantly reduce einsum costs. See Symmetry Savings for details."},"463":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-43","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#quick-tips-for-competition","content":"**Prefer cheaper operations.** A matrix-vector product via `fnp.einsum('ij,j->i', A, x)` costs `m*n` FLOPs, while a full matrix-matrix multiply costs `m*n*k`. Avoid computing more than you need."},"464":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-44","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#quick-tips-for-competition","content":"**Watch out for hidden costs.** Operations like `fnp.array()` and `fnp.concatenate()` are *not* free -- they charge `numel(output)` FLOPs, and `fnp.where()` charges `numel(condition)`. Check the cost of any operation you are unsure about."},"465":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-45","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#local-vs-remote-backends","content":"You develop against the in-process `flopscope`, but AIcrowd runs your code on\nthe remote (client/server) backend. Five callback-taking ops —\n`apply_along_axis`, `apply_over_axes`, `fromfunction`, `fromiter`, `piecewise`\n— work in-process but raise `RemoteCallbackError` remotely, because a Python\ncallback can't be sent over the wire. Calling them in-process now emits a\n`RemoteCallbackWarning`; enumerate the set with\n`flopscope.remote_unsupported_ops()`. Precompute these results or avoid the ops\nin submitted code. Likewise, passing a non-serializable argument (a generator,\nlambda, or custom object) to any op raises `RemoteSerializationError` remotely —\npass materialized arrays or built-ins instead."},"466":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-46","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#immutable-arrays","content":"flopscope arrays are **immutable**, like JAX arrays. Item assignment and indexed in-place updates raise `TypeError`:"},"467":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-47","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#immutable-arrays","content":"The `+=` is a red herring: `layer_sums[i] += value` desugars to\n`layer_sums[i] = layer_sums[i] + value`, so it is the *indexed assignment* that\nfails, not the addition. Whole-array `+=` (no index on the left) is fine."},"468":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-48","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#immutable-arrays","content":"Build results functionally instead. Collect the pieces and stack them once:"},"469":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-49","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#immutable-arrays","content":"...or keep a running total with a whole-array update:"},"470":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-50","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#immutable-arrays","content":"This is also a local-vs-remote difference: the in-process `flopscope` backend is\na NumPy subclass and currently tolerates `arr[i] = ...`, but the remote backend\nAIcrowd grades on rejects it. Write the functional form so local runs and graded\nruns behave identically."},"471":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/competition-51","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/competition","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/competition#when-things-go-wrong","content":"If you hit `BudgetExhaustedError`, see Budget Planning & Debugging for a systematic approach to diagnosing overruns and reducing costs."},"472":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"page","content":"Installation","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Getting Started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation"},"473":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-0","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#install-the-latest-stable-release-pypi","content":"Install the latest stable release (PyPI)"},"474":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-1","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#install-the-bleeding-edge-version-git","content":"Install the bleeding-edge version (git)"},"475":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-2","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#install-for-development","content":"Install for development"},"476":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-3","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#verify-installation","content":"Verify installation"},"477":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-4","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#what-youll-see","content":"What you'll see"},"478":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-5","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#common-pitfalls","content":"Common pitfalls"},"479":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-6","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#related-pages","content":"Related pages"},"480":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-7","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"*Use this page when setting up flopscope for the first time.*"},"481":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-8","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"**You will learn:**"},"482":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-9","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"How to install the latest stable release from PyPI"},"483":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-10","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"How to install the bleeding-edge version directly from git"},"484":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-11","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"How to set up a development install"},"485":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-12","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"How to verify your installation works"},"486":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-13","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation","content":"How to fix common installation pitfalls"},"487":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-14","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#install-the-latest-stable-release-pypi","content":"Use this for most cases. Releases follow Semantic Versioning;\nsee the CHANGELOG\nfor what's in each release."},"488":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-15","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#install-the-bleeding-edge-version-git","content":"Use this if you need a fix or feature that hasn't shipped to PyPI yet.\nAppend `@<ref>` to pin to a branch, tag, or commit — e.g.\n`...flopscope.git@main` or `...flopscope.git@v0.2.0`."},"489":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-16","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#install-for-development","content":"This gives you an editable install plus the `dev`, `docs`, and `sympy` extras."},"490":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-17","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#what-youll-see","content":"The version string includes the installed NumPy version suffix. If you see a version number, flopscope is installed correctly."},"491":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-18","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#common-pitfalls","content":"**Symptom:** `ImportError: numpy version mismatch`"},"492":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-19","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#common-pitfalls","content":"**Fix:** Flopscope supports NumPy `>=2.0.0,<2.3.0` (default install uses NumPy 2.2). Using `uv` handles this automatically. If you installed manually, check your NumPy version:"},"493":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/installation-20","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/installation#related-pages","content":"Quickstart — run your first FLOP-counted computation"},"494":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","type":"page","content":"Quickstart","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Getting Started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart"},"495":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-0","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"496":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-1","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#quickest-possible-start","content":"Quickest possible start"},"497":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-2","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#what-youll-see","content":"What you'll see"},"498":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-3","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"499":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-4","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","content":"*Run your first FLOP-counted computation in under 2 minutes.*"},"500":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-5","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","content":"**You will learn:**"},"501":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-6","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","content":"How to run a FLOP-counted computation with the global default budget"},"502":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-7","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","content":"How to read the budget summary output"},"503":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-8","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#prerequisites","content":"Installation"},"504":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-9","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#quickest-possible-start","content":"You do not need to set up a budget context to start counting FLOPs. flopscope activates a global default context the first time any counted operation runs. The default budget is 1e15 FLOPs (configurable via the `FLOPSCOPE_DEFAULT_BUDGET` environment variable)."},"505":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-10","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#quickest-possible-start","content":"Save this as `first_budget.py`:"},"506":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-11","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#quickest-possible-start","content":"Run it:"},"507":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-12","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#what-youll-see","content":"**Reading the output:**"},"508":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-13","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#what-youll-see","content":"**Top rows:** `Used` is the total FLOP count spent so far across the current session, and `Remaining` shows the implicit global headroom"},"509":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-14","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#what-youll-see","content":"**Flat default:** the summary stays flat unless you explicitly ask for `flops.budget_summary(by_namespace=True)`"},"510":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-15","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#what-youll-see","content":"**Operations table:** the 10-layer MLP is dominated by `random.randn` for weight init (\\~80%) and the per-layer matrix-vector `einsum` (\\~10%); `multiply` and `array` from the Kaiming-scaled init each contribute \\~5%, activations (`maximum`) are comparatively cheap, and the Kaiming scale `sqrt` adds just 1 FLOP"},"511":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-16","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#what-youll-see","content":"If you want namespace attribution, opt in separately:"},"512":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart-17","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/quickstart#next-steps","content":"Ready for budget limits? See the Competition Guide."},"513":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"page","content":"Budget Planning & Debugging","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning"},"514":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#estimate-before-running","content":"Estimate before running"},"515":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#budget-breakdown-example","content":"Budget breakdown example"},"516":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#read-the-budget-summary","content":"Read the budget summary"},"517":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"Add a time limit when FLOPs are not the only risk"},"518":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Diagnose overruns"},"519":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"What to do next"},"520":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","content":"*Estimate costs before running and diagnose overruns after.*"},"521":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","content":"**You will learn:**"},"522":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","content":"How to use cost query functions to estimate FLOPs without executing"},"523":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","content":"How to read and interpret the budget summary"},"524":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","content":"How to diagnose expensive operations using the operation log"},"525":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","content":"Optimization strategies for reducing FLOP consumption"},"526":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#estimate-before-running","content":"Flopscope provides cost query functions that compute FLOP costs from shapes without executing anything or touching the budget. Use these to plan before committing FLOPs:"},"527":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#estimate-before-running","content":"For multi-operand einsums (3+ operands), use `fnp.einsum_path()` to see the step-by-step contraction breakdown with per-step costs and symmetry savings:"},"528":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#estimate-before-running","content":"`fnp.einsum_path()` does not execute the contraction, but it does record a nominal 1-FLOP planning event so the path query itself is still visible in the operation log."},"529":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#budget-breakdown-example","content":"Plan a multi-step computation before executing:"},"530":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#read-the-budget-summary","content":"Call `flops.budget_summary()` after your computation for a human-readable breakdown, or `budget.summary()` inside a context. Pass `by_namespace=True` when you want dotted namespace attribution:"},"531":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#read-the-budget-summary","content":"The summary shows cost per operation type, sorted by highest cost first. Look for operations consuming a disproportionate share of the budget. When you opt into `by_namespace=True`, the display adds a namespace breakdown for the exact dotted paths recorded in that run."},"532":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#read-the-budget-summary","content":"For programmatic analysis, use `flops.budget_summary_dict()`:"},"533":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#read-the-budget-summary","content":"Use `flops.budget_summary_dict(by_namespace=True)` for exact per-namespace breakdowns keyed by the full dotted path:"},"534":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"Some operations are analytically cheap enough to fit the FLOP budget but still\nslow in practice. Use `wall_time_limit_s` on the same `BudgetContext` when you\nwant a cooperative wall-clock deadline in addition to the FLOP cap:"},"535":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"When the time limit is exceeded, Flopscope raises `TimeExhaustedError` at the next\noperation boundary. The summary exposes four timing views that decompose wall time exactly:"},"536":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"`wall_time_s`: total elapsed time for the context"},"537":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"`flopscope_backend_time_s`: time spent inside the underlying NumPy / BLAS / LAPACK backend calls being counted (including data-movement ops like `tile`, `take`, `pad`, `resize`)"},"538":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"`flopscope_overhead_time_s`: time spent in flopscope's own dispatch code (wrapper preambles, FLOP cost computation, view-casts, post-call wrapping, `maybe_check_nan_inf` when opted in via `flopscope.configure(check_nan_inf=True)`)"},"539":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"`residual_wall_time_s`: the measured wall-clock remainder outside backend calls and flopscope overhead (user Python between ops, `time.sleep`, GC pauses, un-instrumented numpy, and the callback time of `apply_along_axis` / `apply_over_axes` / `piecewise` / `fromfunction` / `fromiter`)"},"540":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"The identity `wall_time_s = flopscope_backend_time_s + flopscope_overhead_time_s + residual_wall_time_s` holds within numerical tolerance."},"541":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#add-a-time-limit-when-flops-are-not-the-only-risk","content":"Use `budget.summary()` when you want the current context's timings, and\n`flops.budget_summary()` when you want the accumulated session/global view."},"542":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"When you hit a `BudgetExhaustedError`, the budget's operation log gives per-call detail:"},"543":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Each `OpRecord` contains:"},"544":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Field"},"545":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Description"},"546":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`op_name`"},"547":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Operation name (e.g., `\"einsum\"`, `\"exp\"`)"},"548":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`namespace`"},"549":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Effective namespace path recorded for that operation"},"550":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`subscripts`"},"551":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Einsum subscript string, or `None`"},"552":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`shapes`"},"553":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Tuple of input shapes"},"554":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`flop_cost`"},"555":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"FLOP cost of this single call"},"556":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`cumulative`"},"557":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Running total after this call"},"558":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`flopscope_context_start_offset_s`"},"559":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Seconds from the active `BudgetContext` start to when this operation was recorded"},"560":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`flopscope_backend_duration_s`"},"561":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Seconds spent in the underlying backend call for this operation"},"562":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"`flopscope_overhead_duration_s`"},"563":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Seconds of flopscope wrapper/accounting overhead attributed to this operation"},"564":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"Look for the operation where `cumulative` jumps sharply -- that is your most expensive call."},"565":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#diagnose-overruns","content":"For real-time monitoring during long computations, use the live budget display:"},"566":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-52","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"Once you have identified the expensive operations, apply these strategies:"},"567":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-53","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"**Reduce dimensions.** If `random.randn(1024, 1024)` is too expensive, try smaller arrays. A 512x512 matrix costs 1/4 the FLOPs of a 1024x1024 matrix for a matmul."},"568":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-54","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"**Exploit symmetry.** If operands are symmetric, use `flops.as_symmetric()` to halve pointwise costs and significantly reduce einsum costs. See Symmetry Savings."},"569":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-55","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"**Use cheaper operations.** A matrix-vector product costs `m*n` FLOPs, while a matrix-matrix product costs `m*n*k`. Avoid computing full matrix products when you only need a few rows or a single vector result."},"570":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-56","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"**Increase budget.** If the computation is genuinely needed and you have headroom, raise `flop_budget` on the `BudgetContext`."},"571":{"id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning-57","page_id":"/docs/guides/budget-planning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/budget-planning#what-to-do-next","content":"**Split into phases.** Use namespaces to attribute different phases without splitting the FLOP budget into child budgets:"},"572":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"page","content":"Einsum Patterns","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum"},"573":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"574":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#common-patterns","content":"Common patterns"},"575":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"Cost formula"},"576":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#fnpdot-and-fnpmatmul","content":"fnp.dot and fnp.matmul"},"577":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#symmetric-tensors","content":"Symmetric tensors"},"578":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-costs","content":"Inspecting costs"},"579":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Inspecting what gets charged"},"580":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#custom-contraction-paths","content":"Custom contraction paths"},"581":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#path-caching","content":"Path caching"},"582":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cache-management","content":"Cache management"},"583":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#common-pitfalls","content":"Common pitfalls"},"584":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/einsum#related-pages","content":"Related pages"},"585":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum","content":"*Use this page to understand `fnp.einsum` -- the core computation primitive in flopscope.*"},"586":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum","content":"**You will learn:**"},"587":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum","content":"How to write common einsum patterns and understand their FLOP costs"},"588":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum","content":"How to use symmetric tensors with einsum for cost savings"},"589":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum","content":"How to inspect and customize contraction paths"},"590":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum","content":"How to leverage path caching for repeated operations"},"591":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#prerequisites","content":"Quickstart"},"592":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"The cost of an einsum is the sum of per-step costs along the optimal contraction path. Every einsum — even a simple two-operand one — goes through the opt\\_einsum path optimizer (a symmetry-aware fork of opt\\_einsum)."},"593":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"For each pairwise contraction step the cost decomposes into a multiplicative term `μ` and an accumulation term `α`:"},"594":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"where `μ` is the product of all index dimensions (the FMAs you'd issue) and `α` charges the accumulation across the contracted axes. The α/M model counts multiplies and adds separately (FMA=2 textbook); the factor-of-2 you see for dense matmul comes from `μ` counting multiplies and `α` counting accumulation adds."},"595":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"For `'ij,jk->ik'` with shapes `(256, 256)` and `(256, 256)`:"},"596":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"Indices: i=256, j=256, k=256"},"597":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"μ = 256 × 256 × 256 = 16,777,216"},"598":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"α = 16,777,216"},"599":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"Cost: 33,554,432"},"600":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"For multi-operand einsums (3+ tensors), Flopscope automatically decomposes the contraction into optimal pairwise steps. The total cost is the sum of per-step costs."},"601":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#cost-formula","content":"When symmetric tensors are involved, each step's cost is further reduced by the ratio of unique output elements to total output elements. See Symmetry Savings for the full practical guide."},"602":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#fnpdot-and-fnpmatmul","content":"`fnp.dot(A, B)` and `fnp.matmul(A, B)` are equivalent to the corresponding einsum and have the same FLOP cost."},"603":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#symmetric-tensors","content":"There are two separate symmetry declarations — one for inputs, one for outputs:"},"604":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#symmetric-tensors","content":"**Input symmetry** — wrap with `flops.as_symmetric()` before passing to einsum. The optimizer automatically uses symmetry to choose the best contraction order and charges reduced costs:"},"605":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#symmetric-tensors","content":"**Output symmetry** — pass `symmetry=` to `einsum()` to declare that the result is symmetric. This wraps the output as a `SymmetricTensor` so downstream operations benefit from reduced costs. It does NOT affect the cost of this einsum — it's a declaration about the result's structure:"},"606":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#symmetric-tensors","content":"For the full symmetry guide, see Symmetry Savings."},"607":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-costs","content":"`fnp.einsum_path()` previews the contraction plan without executing the contraction itself. Planning is cheap: it records a nominal 1-FLOP `einsum_path` event, but none of the contraction FLOPs are spent."},"608":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-costs","content":"The printed table shows the contraction order, the α/M cost (path-independent — naive and optimized are equal by construction), largest intermediate, grouped index sizes, and one row per pairwise contraction step. Each step shows the chosen contract tuple, subscript, per-step FLOPs, and BLAS tag."},"609":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-costs","content":"For per-step debugging, call `print(info.format_table(verbose=True))`. The verbose view adds indented rows with the merged operand subset, the intermediate output shape, and the running cumulative cost."},"610":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-costs","content":"For the symmetric-vs-dense FLOP breakdown that the path-independent total doesn't expose, use `flops.einsum_accumulation_cost(...).describe()` — see the inspection section below."},"611":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"If you want to see the exact FLOP cost flopscope will charge for an einsum (without actually running it), call `flops.einsum_accumulation_cost`:"},"612":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"The cost is path-independent: `flops.einsum_accumulation_cost(...).total` matches `info.optimized_cost` from `fnp.einsum_path(...)`, and both equal the FLOPs that `BudgetContext` will charge when you actually call `fnp.einsum(...)`. Repeated calls hit an LRU cache and return in microseconds — see `flops.einsum_cache_info()` and `flops.einsum_clear_caches()` if you need to reset between cold-call benchmarks."},"613":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"For inputs with declared symmetry, the returned total reflects the orbit-aware savings — see Symmetry-aware FLOP counting for the mental model."},"614":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#custom-contraction-paths","content":"By default Flopscope finds the optimal contraction order automatically. You can override this by passing an explicit path — a list of int-tuples specifying which operand positions to contract at each step:"},"615":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#custom-contraction-paths","content":"You can also specify a completely custom path. Each tuple names the positions (in the current operand list) to contract; the result is appended to the end:"},"616":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#custom-contraction-paths","content":"Different paths may have different FLOP costs. Use `fnp.einsum_path()` to compare — it returns the plan without executing the contraction."},"617":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#path-caching","content":"Contraction paths are cached automatically in a module-level LRU cache.\nWhen you call `fnp.einsum()` with the same subscripts, shapes, optimizer,\nand symmetry structure, the path is reused from cache instead of being\nrecomputed. This makes repeated einsums in loops essentially free in\npath-finding overhead:"},"618":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#path-caching","content":"`fnp.einsum_path()` shares the same cache, so planning a path warms the\ncache for subsequent `fnp.einsum()` calls and vice versa."},"619":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#common-pitfalls","content":"**Symptom:** Unexpectedly high FLOP cost"},"620":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#common-pitfalls","content":"**Fix:** Check all index dimensions. A subscript like `'ijkl,jklm->im'` multiplies all five dimension sizes together. Use `flops.accounting.einsum_cost()` or `fnp.einsum_path()` to preview costs before executing."},"621":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#related-pages","content":"Symmetry Savings — full guide to symmetry mechanisms"},"622":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#related-pages","content":"API Reference — algorithms, symmetry support, and operation details"},"623":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#related-pages","content":"Plan Your Budget — query costs before executing"},"624":{"id":"/docs/guides/einsum-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/einsum","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/einsum#related-pages","content":"FLOP Counting Model — how costs are computed"},"625":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"page","content":"FFT Operations","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft"},"626":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"627":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft#cost-model","content":"Cost model"},"628":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft#basic-usage","content":"Basic usage"},"629":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft#real-vs-complex-transforms","content":"Real vs complex transforms"},"630":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft#multi-dimensional-fft","content":"Multi-dimensional FFT"},"631":{"id":"/docs/guides/fft-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/fft","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/fft#windowed