e"},"810":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Works anywhere too"},"811":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"A global default budget auto-activates; use explicit `BudgetContext` for limits and namespacing"},"812":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"`np.linalg.svd(A)`"},"813":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"`fnp.linalg.svd(A, k=10)`"},"814":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Truncated SVD with explicit `k`"},"815":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Plain `ndarray` only"},"816":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"`SymmetricTensor` available"},"817":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Wrap with `flops.as_symmetric()` for cost savings"},"818":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"All NumPy ops available"},"819":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Most available, 32 blacklisted"},"820":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"I/O and config ops raise `AttributeError`"},"821":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"No cost tracking"},"822":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Automatic FLOP counting"},"823":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#what-changes","content":"Every counted op deducts from budget"},"824":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#common-pitfalls","content":"**Symptom:** `AttributeError` when calling an I/O or config function (e.g., `fnp.save`, `fnp.seterr`)"},"825":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#common-pitfalls","content":"**Fix:** 32 operations are blacklisted because they are I/O, configuration, or datetime functions with no FLOP cost. See Operation Categories for the full list. Use `numpy` directly for these."},"826":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#common-pitfalls","content":"**Symptom:** Using `np.linalg.svd` instead of `fnp.linalg.svd`"},"827":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#common-pitfalls","content":"**Fix:** If you import NumPy alongside flopscope, make sure to use `fnp.` for counted operations. Operations called through `np.` bypass FLOP counting entirely."},"828":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#related-pages","content":"Operation Categories — what's supported and what isn't"},"829":{"id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/migrate-from-numpy#related-pages","content":"API Reference — full list of all operations"},"830":{"id":"/docs/guides/random","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"page","content":"Random Number Generation","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random"},"831":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#tldr","content":"TL;DR"},"832":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"Three ways to sample"},"833":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#default_rng--canonical-pattern","content":"`default_rng()` — canonical pattern"},"834":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#randomstate--legacy-compat","content":"`RandomState()` — legacy compat"},"835":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#module-level-samplers","content":"Module-level samplers"},"836":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#cross-api-parity","content":"Cross-API parity"},"837":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#state-management","content":"State management"},"838":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#pickle-and-copy","content":"Pickle and copy"},"839":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#spawn-for-parallel-work","content":"`spawn()` for parallel work"},"840":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#constructing-from-a-specific-bitgenerator","content":"Constructing from a specific BitGenerator"},"841":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"Pitfall: global state"},"842":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#forward-compatibility-integration-with-other-apis","content":"Forward compatibility: integration with other APIs"},"843":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"What's deliberately not supported"},"844":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"Related"},"845":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"*Counted RNGs for reproducible, FLOP-tracked sampling.*"},"846":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"**You will learn:**"},"847":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"The three ways to draw random samples in flopscope and when to use each"},"848":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"How `default_rng()` is the canonical, recommended pattern"},"849":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"How counted RNGs behave under pickle/copy/spawn"},"850":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"How they integrate with downstream APIs (e.g. future `flopscope.stats.rvs()`)"},"851":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random","content":"The one foot-gun left over from numpy's legacy API"},"852":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#tldr","content":"This is the **canonical pattern**: per-call seed, per-instance state, fully isolated, FLOP-counted."},"853":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"API"},"854":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"Status"},"855":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"Per-instance state?"},"856":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"Use when"},"857":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"`fnp.random.default_rng(seed)`"},"858":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"**canonical** ✅"},"859":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"yes"},"860":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"always (modern code)"},"861":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"`fnp.random.RandomState(seed)`"},"862":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"first-class"},"863":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"yes"},"864":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"porting code from numpy's legacy API"},"865":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"`fnp.random.randn(...)`, `normal(...)`, etc."},"866":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"first-class"},"867":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"shares numpy's global RNG"},"868":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"short scripts, REPL exploration"},"869":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"All three charge FLOPs to the active `BudgetContext`. The difference is *where the random state lives*."},"870":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#default_rng--canonical-pattern","content":"The returned object is a `numpy.random.Generator` subclass — `isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator)` holds, so it works anywhere a numpy `Generator` is expected."},"871":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#randomstate--legacy-compat","content":"The legacy `numpy.random.RandomState` API is still supported with full FLOP counting:"},"872":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#randomstate--legacy-compat","content":"Use this only when you're porting code that already uses `RandomState`. New code should prefer `default_rng()`."},"873":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#module-level-samplers","content":"`fnp.random.randn`, `normal`, `uniform`, `choice`, `shuffle`, etc. are kept first-class for short scripts and REPL use:"},"874":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#module-level-samplers","content":"These match `numpy.random` semantics exactly, plus FLOP accounting. **They share numpy's global RNG state**, which is fine for one-shot scripts but creates a foot-gun in longer-running programs — see Pitfall: global state below."},"875":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#cross-api-parity","content":"Same physical operation charges identical FLOPs regardless of which API you used:"},"876":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#cross-api-parity","content":"`default_rng().normal(...)` inherits the calibrated weight of the module-level `random.normal` it shadows. No per-method calibration is needed for new method-level entries — runtime aliasing handles it."},"877":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pickle-and-copy","content":"Counted RNGs round-trip cleanly through `pickle` and `copy`:"},"878":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#spawn-for-parallel-work","content":"`Generator.spawn(n)` returns `n` independent counted children that share no state with the parent:"},"879":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#spawn-for-parallel-work","content":"Each child has its own `bit_generator`, so children running in parallel produce independent streams — no contention, no shared state."},"880":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#constructing-from-a-specific-bitgenerator","content":"The `Generator(BitGenerator(seed))` idiom from numpy works identically:"},"881":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#constructing-from-a-specific-bitgenerator","content":"The bit-generator classes (`BitGenerator`, `MT19937`, `PCG64`, `PCG64DXSM`, `Philox`, `SFC64`) pass through to numpy unchanged — they don't do math, so there's nothing to count at the bit-generator level. FLOPs are charged at the sampler-method level on the resulting `Generator`."},"882":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"The one path that *doesn't* give per-instance isolation is the module-level samplers, because they share numpy's global RNG. Across two sequential `BudgetContext`s:"},"883":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-52","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"If you need each context to be independently reproducible, either re-seed at the top of each context, or use `default_rng()`:"},"884":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-53","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"Note that **`BudgetContext`s cannot be nested** at all (the runtime raises `RuntimeError(\"Cannot nest BudgetContexts\")`), so the global-state issue only affects *sequential* contexts, never overlapping ones."},"885":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-54","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#forward-compatibility-integration-with-other-apis","content":"Anything that accepts a numpy `Generator` accepts a counted `Generator`. For example, when `flopscope.stats` grows an `rvs()` method (random variate sampling), it will accept a counted RNG and inherit FLOP counting for free:"},"886":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-55","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#forward-compatibility-integration-with-other-apis","content":"The pattern works because `rng.uniform(...)` and `fnp.stats.norm.ppf(...)` are both already FLOP-counted. Inverse-CDF sampling threads naturally through the existing wrappers."},"887":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-56","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Pattern"},"888":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-57","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Status"},"889":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-58","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Why"},"890":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-59","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Nested `BudgetContext`s"},"891":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-60","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"runtime error"},"892":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-61","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Out of scope; flopscope models a single budget per program region."},"893":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-62","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Methods not in the registry"},"894":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-63","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"`UnsupportedFunctionError`"},"895":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-64","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Closes the silent-bypass hole from issue #18. New numpy methods are added to the registry on each version bump (CI: `scripts/numpy_audit.py --ci`)."},"896":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-65","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"`numpy.random.<X>` directly"},"897":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-66","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"not counted"},"898":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-67","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Use `fnp.random.<X>` for counting. `numpy.random.X` continues to work but bypasses flopscope."},"899":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-68","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"`fnp.random.default_rng` — canonical entry point (op page)"},"900":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-69","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"Migrate from NumPy — broader migration guide"},"901":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-70","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"Issue #18 — design rationale and history"},"902":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-71","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"(`fnp.random.RandomState` is a class constructor, not a registered op — its\nsampler methods have individual op pages under `/docs/api/numpy/random/`.)"},"903":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"page","content":"Saving & Loading Models","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load"},"904":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"The mental model"},"905":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Saving and loading arrays"},"906":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#custom-models-with-flopsmodule","content":"Custom models with flops.Module"},"907":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"How `state_dict` auto-discovery works"},"908":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#config-and-reconstruction","content":"`config()` and reconstruction"},"909":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#in-place-loading-with-load","content":"In-place loading with `load`"},"910":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#authoring-weight-files-offline","content":"Authoring weight files offline"},"911":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"FAQ: why can't `load` return my model object directly?"},"912":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"*Store weights in a portable, pickle-free file. Loading them back is free; saving bills the bytes you write.*"},"913":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"**You will learn:**"},"914":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"What a flopscope file contains and why it is safe to load"},"915":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"How to save and load individual arrays with `fnp.save`/`fnp.savez`/`fnp.load`"},"916":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"How to build a custom model with `flops.Module` and round-trip it through a file"},"917":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"How to prepare weight files offline with plain NumPy and load them during the challenge run"},"918":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"A flopscope file stores exactly two things:"},"919":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"**Named numeric arrays** — floating-point, integer, bool, or complex; no object dtype."},"920":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"**An inert JSON `__meta__` block** — plain key/value data, never executed."},"921":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"Your *class* always lives in *your code*, never in the file. flopscope reads the numeric payload and hands the values to the object you construct — no pickle, no `eval`, no `exec`. That boundary is the entire security model: loading a file can never execute code."},"922":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"Loading is **free**: `fnp.load` charges 0 FLOPs regardless of file size. Saving is\nnot — `fnp.save`, `fnp.savez`, and `fnp.savez_compressed` each charge 4 FLOPs per\nelement written, plus 4 FLOPs per byte of the array names themselves and of a\nserialized `__meta__` block, so there is no free way to smuggle data through any of\nthose channels."},"923":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Use `fnp.savez` to write multiple named arrays into a single `.npz` file:"},"924":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Load them back with `fnp.load`, which returns a plain `dict`:"},"925":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"For a single array, use the `.npy` variants:"},"926":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Use `fnp.savez_compressed` when file size matters — it writes the same format as\n`fnp.savez` but with lossless compression:"},"927":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"> **Note:** object-dtype arrays (`dtype=object`) are rejected at save time.\n> Only numeric dtypes (float, int, bool, complex) are accepted."},"928":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#custom-models-with-flopsmodule","content":"For whole models, `flops.Module` auto-discovers every array attribute and\nnested sub-module, so you never write a manual save loop."},"929":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`flops.Module.state_dict()` walks `vars(self)` and collects:"},"930":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Attribute type"},"931":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"How it is collected"},"932":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"933":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"stored directly under the attribute name"},"934":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Nested `flops.Module`"},"935":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"recursed with a dotted prefix (`layers.0.W`)"},"936":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`list` / `tuple` of the above"},"937":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"indexed as `layers.0`, `layers.1`, …"},"938":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`dict` of the above"},"939":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"keyed as `config.key`"},"940":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Attributes starting with `_`"},"941":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"**skipped**"},"942":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Everything else (scalars, strings, …)"},"943":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"**skipped**"},"944":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#config-and-reconstruction","content":"Override `config()` to return the keyword arguments that `__init__` needs to\nreconstruct your model with the right shapes. `from_file` passes them verbatim:"},"945":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#in-place-loading-with-load","content":"If you already have a model with the right architecture, call `load` to update\nits weights in place:"},"946":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#authoring-weight-files-offline","content":"You can prepare weight files in your regular development environment (real NumPy,\nGPU frameworks, etc.) and load them inside the budgeted competition run:"},"947":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#authoring-weight-files-offline","content":"`fnp.load` reads files written by plain `numpy.save`/`numpy.savez` without any\nformat conversion. The only constraint is that the arrays must be numeric dtype."},"948":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"Returning a live model object from a file would require encoding the *class* into\nthe file. The only general way to do that is pickle — and pickle can execute\narbitrary code at load time. flopscope deliberately avoids this."},"949":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"Instead, the class always comes from *your code*:"},"950":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"`from_file` is a class method: you call it on `MLP`, so Python already knows\nthe class. The file only supplies the weights (numeric arrays) and the\nJSON config. No code ever travels through the file."},"951":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"page","content":"Symmetry Savings","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry"},"952":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Why symmetry matters"},"953":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"954":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#how-to-declare-symmetry","content":"How to declare symmetry"},"955":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#full-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"Full symmetry with `SymmetryGroup.symmetric`"},"956":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-independent-full-symmetric-groups","content":"Multiple independent full symmetric groups"},"957":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#explicit-full-symmetric-groups-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"Explicit full symmetric groups with `SymmetryGroup.symmetric`"},"958":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cyclic-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupcyclic","content":"Cyclic symmetry with `SymmetryGroup.cyclic`"},"959":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#dihedral-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupdihedral","content":"Dihedral symmetry with `SymmetryGroup.dihedral`"},"960":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#arbitrary-subgroups-from-custom-generators","content":"Arbitrary subgroups from custom generators"},"961":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-explicit-groups-on-one-tensor","content":"Multiple explicit groups on one tensor"},"962":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"Generating example data with the Reynolds operator"},"963":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Symmetry propagation at a glance"},"964":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"Slicing rules"},"965":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"Reduction rules"},"966":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Binary pointwise ops and broadcasting"},"967":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"Warnings, conservative behavior, and re-tagging"},"968":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#edge-cases-when-symmetrictensors-meet-numpy-protocols","content":"Edge cases when SymmetricTensors meet NumPy protocols"},"969":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"In-place dunders refuse symmetry-corrupting writes"},"970":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-sort--partition-refuse-on-symmetrictensor","content":"In-place sort / partition refuse on SymmetricTensor"},"971":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncat-refuses-on-symmetrictensor","content":"`ufunc.at` refuses on SymmetricTensor"},"972":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncouter-produces-direct-product-symmetry","content":"`ufunc.outer` produces direct-product symmetry"},"973":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#tensordot-keeps-surviving-direct-product-symmetry","content":"`tensordot` keeps surviving direct-product symmetry"},"974":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multi-output-ufuncs-preserve-symmetry-on-every-output","content":"Multi-output ufuncs preserve symmetry on every output"},"975":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"Cost model is a placeholder above the `dimino_budget` group order"},"976":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"Under the hood"},"977":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#going-deeper","content":"Going deeper"},"978":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"*Reduce FLOP costs when your tensors have symmetry.*"},"979":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"**You will learn:**"},"980":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"How to declare full and non-full tensor symmetries with `flops.as_symmetric()`"},"981":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"How to generate example tensors for arbitrary permutation groups"},"982":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"When to use `fnp.random.symmetric()` (sample + project) versus `flops.symmetrize()`\n(project existing data)"},"983":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"How slicing, reductions, and binary pointwise ops preserve, weaken, or drop symmetry metadata"},"984":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"When to re-tag results with `flops.as_symmetric()` after conservative propagation"},"985":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"> For the underlying mental model — orbits, unique work items, why these savings exist — see Symmetry-aware FLOP counting."},"986":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Many tensors contain repeated structure. A symmetric matrix has only\n`n * (n + 1) / 2` unique elements instead of `n^2`, and higher-order tensors\nwith permutation symmetry can shrink the effective element count even more.\nWhen Flopscope knows a tensor's symmetry, it charges FLOPs based on unique elements\ninstead of dense ones."},"987":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Operation"},"988":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Dense cost"},"989":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Symmetry-aware cost"},"990":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Why it drops"},"991":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.exp(s2_matrix)`"},"992":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`n^2`"},"993":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`n * (n + 1) / 2`"},"994":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"only unique matrix entries matter"},"995":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.einsum('ki,kj->ij', x, x)` with `x` repeated, `m=n=4`"},"996":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"128"},"997":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"80"},"998":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"the repeated `x` operand lets flopscope detect symmetric output and discount accumulations + multiplications"},"999":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.einsum('i,j,k->ijk', v, v, v)` with `v` repeated, `n=4`"},"1000":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"192"},"1001":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"60"},"1002":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"three identical operands induce an `S₃` action on the output labels"},"1003":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.sum(s3_tensor)` on a fully-symmetric `(4, 4, 4)`"},"1004":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-52","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"63"},"1005":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-53","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"19"},"1006":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-54","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"the same orbit logic applies to reductions, not just einsum"},"1007":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-55","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"By contrast, declaring `symmetry=` on an `einsum` output tags the result for downstream\noperations; it does not reduce that `einsum`'s own cost by itself."},"1008":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-56","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#quick-start","content":"`flops.as_symmetric()` validates the data first. After that, Flopscope propagates\nsymmetry metadata algebraically through many operations. Unary pointwise ops preserve symmetry-aware costs and keep the same exact group, including non-full groups such as `C_k` or `D_k`. Slicing, reductions, and binary pointwise ops can weaken it or remove it entirely."},"1009":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-57","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#full-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"Use `SymmetryGroup.symmetric` when the tensor is invariant under every permutation of a\nset of axes."},"1010":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-58","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#full-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"This is the most common declaration: full `S_2` symmetry on matrix axes\n`(0, 1)`."},"1011":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-59","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-independent-full-symmetric-groups","content":"This declares one full symmetric group on axes `(0, 1)` and another on\n`(2, 3)`."},"1012":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-60","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#explicit-full-symmetric-groups-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"This explicit group is useful once you want to inspect or combine symmetry directly."},"1013":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-61","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cyclic-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupcyclic","content":"`C_3` means rotations are allowed, but reflections are not. This is weaker\nthan `S_3`, so it usually gives fewer savings."},"1014":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-62","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#dihedral-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupdihedral","content":"`D_4` includes both rotations and reflections of a four-position structure."},"1015":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-63","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#arbitrary-subgroups-from-custom-generators","content":"Use this form when the built-in constructors do not describe your symmetry."},"1016":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-64","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-explicit-groups-on-one-tensor","content":"This uses a direct product of two exact symmetry factors, one on `(0, 1)` and one on `(2, 3)`."},"1017":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-65","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"When you want example tensors for arbitrary groups, prefer `fnp.random.symmetric`.\nIt is a handy helper that samples from a distribution and applies the Reynolds\noperator.\nUse `flops.symmetrize` when you already have concrete data to symmetrize, and\n`flops.as_symmetric` when you already have concrete data to validate and tag."},"1018":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-66","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`R_G(T) = (1 / |G|) * sum_{g in G} g · T`"},"1019":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-67","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"This helper is ideal for docs, tests, and experiments:"},"1020":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-68","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"it works for `S_k`, `C_k`, `D_k`, and custom generator sets"},"1021":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-69","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"prefer `fnp.random.symmetric()` for synthetic data generation"},"1022":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-70","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`fnp.random.symmetric` internally samples data and calls `flops.symmetrize` so the\nprojection and validation behavior is identical."},"1023":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-71","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"approximate costs (meaningful estimate):"},"1024":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-72","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`fnp.random.symmetric`: `C_dist(n) + |G| * n + n` + validation"},"1025":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-73","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`flops.symmetrize`: `|G| * n + n` + validation\\\nwith `n` total elements and `|G|` group order."},"1026":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-74","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"in exact arithmetic it projects onto the invariant subspace, and in practice\n`flops.as_symmetric()` validates the result with its usual validation tolerances"},"1027":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-75","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"it keeps examples consistent across symmetry classes"},"1028":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-76","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"The propagation examples below assume `import flopscope as flops` plus `import flopscope.numpy as fnp`; generated tensors\nabove use `fnp.random.symmetric(...)` and the explicit transform helper is\n`flops.symmetrize(...)`."},"1029":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-77","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Symmetry propagation is conservative. Flopscope keeps symmetry metadata only when\nthe operation's structure guarantees that the output still respects the\nsurviving group."},"1030":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-78","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Operation"},"1031":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-79","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Result type"},"1032":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-80","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Rule"},"1033":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-81","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.exp(s3_tensor)`"},"1034":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-82","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1035":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-83","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"unary pointwise ops preserve the exact declared group"},"1036":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-84","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.add(s3_tensor, c3_tensor)`"},"1037":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-85","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor` or `FlopscopeArray`"},"1038":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-86","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"binary pointwise ops keep the intersection of both operands' groups"},"1039":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-87","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix * 3`"},"1040":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-88","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1041":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-89","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"scalar binary ops preserve the tensor's groups"},"1042":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-90","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[0]`"},"1043":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-91","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1044":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-92","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"integer indexing removes one axis; no nontrivial group survives"},"1045":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-93","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[:3, :3]`"},"1046":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-94","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1047":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-95","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"equal-size slices can preserve symmetry"},"1048":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-96","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[:3, :2]`"},"1049":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-97","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1050":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-98","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"unequal-size slices break symmetry between those axes"},"1051":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-99","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[fnp.array([0, 1])]`"},"1052":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-100","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1053":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-101","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"advanced indexing drops symmetry conservatively"},"1054":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-102","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.sum(s3_tensor, axis=0)`"},"1055":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-103","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1056":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-104","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"reductions keep the surviving setwise stabilizer subgroup"},"1057":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-105","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.sum(d4_tensor, axis=(1, 3))`"},"1058":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-106","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1059":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-107","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"reductions can keep a proper subgroup like `C_2`"},"1060":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-108","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix @ s2_matrix`"},"1061":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-109","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1062":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-110","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"matrix products are not assumed symmetric in general"},"1063":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-111","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.exp(c3_tensor)` keeps the original `C_3` subgroup exactly; the same applies\nto `D_k` and custom exact groups."},"1064":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-112","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"Slicing uses the **pointwise stabilizer** of the removed axes. Informally:\nevery removed axis must stay fixed under any surviving group element."},"1065":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-113","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"What happens here:"},"1066":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-114","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`same_size_slice` stays symmetric because both surviving axes still have the\nsame size"},"1067":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-115","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`different_size_slice` loses symmetry because the two axes no longer match"},"1068":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-116","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`expanded_s2_matrix` keeps the same `S_2` action, but the axes are renumbered\nfrom `(0, 1)` to `(1, 2)`"},"1069":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-117","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`advanced_index_slice` returns a dense `FlopscopeArray`; flopscope does not attempt to\npropagate symmetry through array/list indexing"},"1070":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-118","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`Ellipsis` behaves like NumPy's normal expansion rules. It can change which\naxes remain, but it does not change the propagation rule itself."},"1071":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-119","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"The difference between full and non-full groups matters immediately:"},"1072":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-120","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`s3_slice` keeps an `S_2` subgroup on the surviving axes"},"1073":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-121","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`c3_slice` loses all nontrivial symmetry, because `C_3` has no non-identity\nelement that fixes one point"},"1074":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-122","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"Reductions use the **setwise stabilizer** of the reduced axes. Informally:\nreduced axes are allowed to permute among themselves, because summation treats\nall positions along the reduced set equivalently."},"1075":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-123","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"What happens here:"},"1076":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-124","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`s3_reduced` keeps an `S_2` subgroup on the remaining axes"},"1077":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-125","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`c3_reduced` loses all nontrivial symmetry"},"1078":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-126","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`c4_reduced` keeps a `C_2` subgroup"},"1079":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-127","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`c4_keepdims` keeps the same surviving subgroup, but the output axes stay in\ntheir original tensor positions because `keepdims=True`"},"1080":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-128","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"Reducing an axis that is not in a symmetry group leaves that group alone, apart\nfrom any axis renumbering caused by the removed dimension."},"1081":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-129","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Binary pointwise ops keep only the symmetry present in **both** operands. For\ngeneral groups, that means element-set intersection on matching output axes,\nnot just matching tuples of axis numbers."},"1082":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-130","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"`intersection_tensor` keeps `C_3`, because `C_3` is the common subgroup of\n`S_3` and `C_3`."},"1083":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-131","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Multiple groups are handled independently:"},"1084":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-132","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"`shared_group_tensor` keeps only the swap on `(0, 1)`. In the exact-group\nrepresentation that surviving action is still embedded in the full output rank,\nso the group's support tuple spans `(0, 1, 2, 3)` even though it acts\nnontrivially only on `(0, 1)`."},"1085":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-133","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Broadcasting matters too:"},"1086":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-134","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Before group intersection, any axis stretched from size `1` to a larger output\nsize is removed from the carried candidate group. So singleton broadcasting by\nitself does not preserve symmetry. In this example, though, `plain_tensor`\nalready carries the same analytically provable `S_2` symmetry on `(0, 1)`, so\n`broadcast_sum` keeps that shared block."},"1087":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-135","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"Flopscope propagates symmetry metadata conservatively. When the operation does not\nguarantee that the declared symmetry survives, the result falls back to a dense\n`FlopscopeArray` with no `.symmetry` metadata."},"1088":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-136","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"`row_slice` is a plain dense `FlopscopeArray`, not a `SymmetricTensor`. That is expected."},"1089":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-137","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"`SymmetryLossWarning` tells you that metadata was dropped or weakened during an\noperation. If you know more about the result than the conservative propagation\nrule does, you can re-tag it with `flops.as_symmetric()`."},"1090":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-138","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"One important caveat: the current implementation does **not** report every\npossible partial weakening via `SymmetryLossWarning`. Treat the warning system\nas helpful guidance, not as a complete audit of every same-axis subgroup\nchange."},"1091":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-139","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#edge-cases-when-symmetrictensors-meet-numpy-protocols","content":"Once your tensors flow through arbitrary user code (or third-party libraries), they\ninevitably hit NumPy's `__array_ufunc__` and `__array_function__` machinery —\n`np.add(A, B)`, `np.divmod(A, B)`, `np.add.outer(A, B)`, `np.add.at(A, idx, vals)`,\n`A @= B`, `np.tensordot(A, B, axes=...)`, and so on. Flopscope's\nprotocol implementations are conservative: when an operation would silently\ncorrupt your declared symmetry, flopscope **refuses or strips** rather than letting\nit through. This section is a tour of those edge cases."},"1092":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-140","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"The right-hand-side has no symmetry, so `A_sym + B_plain` returns a plain\n`FlopscopeArray`. Writing that result back into `A_sym`'s buffer would leave the\nmetadata claiming symmetry while the data is asymmetric. Flopscope refuses with\n`ValueError: in-place add on a SymmetricTensor would weaken or destroy the\ndeclared symmetry`."},"1093":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-141","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"If you know the asymmetric write is intentional, downgrade first:"},"1094":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-142","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"The same guard applies to `__isub__`, `__imul__`, `__itruediv__`,\n`__ifloordiv__`, `__imod__`, `__ipow__`, `__iand__`, `__ior__`, `__ixor__`,\n`__ilshift__`, `__irshift__`, and `__imatmul__` (which additionally falls back\nto CPython's rebind-the-name semantics when the matmul output shape differs\nfrom `self.shape`)."},"1095":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-143","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-sort--partition-refuse-on-symmetrictensor","content":"A reorder along any axis breaks the permutation invariance. Use the out-of-place\nforms instead:"},"1096":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-144","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncat-refuses-on-symmetrictensor","content":"`np.add.at(a, indices, values)` does an unbuffered fancy-index write — every\nrepeat of an index applies again (unlike `a[indices] += values` which dedupes).\nOn a `SymmetricTensor` this almost certainly breaks symmetry, so flopscope\nrefuses:"},"1097":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-145","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncat-refuses-on-symmetrictensor","content":"Downgrade with `A_sym.view(fnp.ndarray)` first if you really need the unbuffered\nupdate."},"1098":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-146","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncouter-produces-direct-product-symmetry","content":"When both operands are symmetric, the output of `np.<ufunc>.outer(A, B)`\ninherits the **direct product** of the input symmetries — `A`'s symmetry on\nits own axes, `B`'s symmetry on the lifted slots `A.ndim..A.ndim+B.ndim-1`:"},"1099":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-147","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#tensordot-keeps-surviving-direct-product-symmetry","content":"The contracted axes drop out of each operand's symmetry; what's left of each\noperand's symmetry on the surviving (uncontracted) axes is direct-producted:"},"1100":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-148","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#tensordot-keeps-surviving-direct-product-symmetry","content":"If the contracted axis is part of the symmetry group, that group is destroyed\non the corresponding side."},"1101":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-149","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multi-output-ufuncs-preserve-symmetry-on-every-output","content":"`np.divmod(A, B)`, `np.frexp(A)`, `np.modf(A)` are elementwise — both outputs\ninherit the same symmetry as their inputs:"},"1102":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-150","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multi-output-ufuncs-preserve-symmetry-on-every-output","content":"`out=(o1, o2)` works (per-slot identity is preserved), and `out=(o1, None)`\nlets numpy allocate just the second slot."},"1103":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-151","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"Flopscope charges `dense_cost × unique_output_elements / dense_output_elements` for\n`ufunc.outer` and `tensordot` as a coarse proxy for the savings a\nsymmetry-aware implementation could realise. The underlying NumPy call still\ndoes dense work; only the budget reflects the savings."},"1104":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-152","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"For a `SymmetryGroup` whose order `|G|` exceeds the configured `dimino_budget`\n(default **50,000** — accepts up to `|S_8| = 40,320` with a small margin),\nflopscope skips this adjustment and charges the full dense cost. Burnside\nenumeration on the group would otherwise iterate every element. The skip is\nannounced via `CostFallbackWarning`:"},"1105":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-153","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"This is rare in practice — common user tensors have small group orders — but\nhigh-rank auto-inferred symmetries on degenerate shapes (`(1,)*N` for large `N`)\ntrip the cap. The warning fires once per `(op_name, |G|)` pair per process to\navoid log flooding. Suppress with `flops.configure(symmetry_warnings=False)`,\nwhich shares the flag with `SymmetryLossWarning`."},"1106":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-154","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"The gate keys on `|G|` rather than degree because `|G|` is the actual driver\nof enumeration cost. A high-degree but small-order group like\n`SymmetryGroup.cyclic(axes=tuple(range(50)))` (degree 50, `|G| = 50`) passes\nthe gate easily, whereas `SymmetryGroup.symmetric(axes=tuple(range(9)))`\n(degree 9, `|G| = 362,880`) bails. For known group families\n(`symmetric`, `cyclic`, `dihedral`, `young`, `direct_product`) `order()` is\nclosed-form O(1), so the check itself never enumerates. Tune the budget with\n`flops.configure(dimino_budget=...)`, or run\n`python -m benchmarks._perm_group_calibration` for a recommendation tied to\nyour host machine."},"1107":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-155","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"A proper algorithmic-cost model is a follow-up."},"1108":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-156","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"The propagation rules are easier to predict if you keep three ideas in mind:"},"1109":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-157","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Unary pointwise ops** preserve symmetry-aware costs, but for\nfull and non-full groups alike they keep the same exact group"},"1110":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-158","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Slicing** uses the pointwise stabilizer of the removed axes"},"1111":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-159","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Reductions** use the setwise stabilizer of the reduced axes"},"1112":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-160","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Binary pointwise ops** use intersection of both operands' groups after\nbroadcast alignment"},"1113":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-161","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"After computing the surviving subgroup, flopscope restricts it to the axes still\npresent in the output and remaps those axes to the output tensor's numbering."},"1114":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-162","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"That is why:"},"1115":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-163","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"slicing one axis of `S_3` leaves `S_2`"},"1116":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-164","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"slicing one axis of `C_3` leaves nothing nontrivial"},"1117":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-165","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"reducing `{1, 3}` of `C_4` can still leave `C_2`"},"1118":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-166","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#going-deeper","content":"Einsum Patterns — how declared and induced symmetry interact with `fnp.einsum`"},"1119":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-167","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#going-deeper","content":"Symmetry Detection Deep Dive — the full detection algorithm for `einsum`"},"1120":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"page","content":"Client-Server Model","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Infrastructure"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server"},"1121":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-0","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#why-client-server","content":"Why client-server?"},"1122":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-1","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"1123":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-2","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#communication-protocol","content":"Communication protocol"},"1124":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-3","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#api-compatibility","content":"API compatibility"},"1125":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-4","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#when-to-use-which","content":"When to use which"},"1126":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-5","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#three-packages-in-this-repo","content":"Three packages in this repo"},"1127":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-6","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#related-pages","content":"Related pages"},"1128":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-7","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","content":"This page covers the client-server architecture used for **competition evaluation**, where participant code runs in an isolated container. For how Flopscope wraps NumPy internally, see How Flopscope Works."},"1129":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-8","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","content":"*Use this page to understand how Flopscope's client-server architecture works and why it exists.*"},"1130":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-9","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","content":"**You will learn:**"},"1131":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-10","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","content":"Why Flopscope uses a client-server model for competition evaluation"},"1132":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-11","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","content":"How arrays, operations, and budgets flow between client and server"},"1133":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-12","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","content":"How to choose between the local library and client-server packages"},"1134":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-13","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#why-client-server","content":"In competition evaluation, participant code runs in an **isolated container** that cannot import NumPy directly. This prevents participants from bypassing FLOP counting by calling NumPy functions outside flopscope."},"1135":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-14","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#why-client-server","content":"The client-server model enforces this isolation:"},"1136":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-15","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#how-it-works","content":"**Server** runs the real flopscope library backed by NumPy. It stores all arrays, enforces budgets, and counts FLOPs."},"1137":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-16","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#how-it-works","content":"**Client** exposes the same public imports (`import flopscope as flops` plus `import flopscope.numpy as fnp`) and proxies every operation to the server over ZMQ (msgpack-encoded messages)."},"1138":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-17","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#how-it-works","content":"**Arrays stay on the server.** The client holds lightweight `RemoteArray` handles that reference server-side data. When you call `fnp.einsum(...)`, the client sends the operation and handle IDs to the server, which executes it and returns a new handle."},"1139":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-18","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#how-it-works","content":"**Budget enforcement happens server-side.** The client cannot manipulate FLOP counts."},"1140":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-19","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#communication-protocol","content":"**Transport:** ZMQ (REQ/REP pattern)"},"1141":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-20","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#communication-protocol","content":"**Serialization:** msgpack with binary-safe array payloads"},"1142":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-21","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#communication-protocol","content":"**Default endpoint:** `ipc:///tmp/flopscope.sock` (configurable via `FLOPSCOPE_SERVER_URL`)"},"1143":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-22","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#communication-protocol","content":"**Timeout:** 30 seconds per request"},"1144":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-23","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#api-compatibility","content":"Code written for the local library works unchanged with the client:"},"1145":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-24","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#when-to-use-which","content":"Use case"},"1146":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-25","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#when-to-use-which","content":"Package"},"1147":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server-26","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/client-server#when-to-use-which","content":"Install path"},"1148":{"id":"/