standing/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"Counting (diff, ediff1d, clip, allclose, isclose, count_nonzero, trace)"},"1248":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-39","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"Copy and gather"},"1249":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-40","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"Copy-and-gather: ops with distinct charged siblings"},"1250":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-41","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"Index generators"},"1251":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-42","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"Boundary ops (free behavior + a value-computing path)"},"1252":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-43","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"I/O (save / load)"},"1253":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-44","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"Functional / higher-order"},"1254":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-45","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"View / free (weight 0.0)"},"1255":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-46","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"Exhaustive per-op reference"},"1256":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-47","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-model-reference","content":"> **Start here.** This is the cost model's conceptual and audit reference. Read it\n> to understand *how* billing works and to satisfy yourself that it is correct and\n> non-gameable — you do **not** need to read every operation. The exhaustive,\n> generated per-op list (every op with its `cost_formula` and `weight`) lives in\n> `ops.json` and the website API pages; this doc\n> explains the model by **family rule** so you can reason about a whole class at once."},"1257":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-48","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-model-reference","content":"flopscope bills compute as:"},"1258":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-49","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Billing model & design principles** — the one equation and *why* it is split into four factors."},"1259":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-50","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Dtype and precision** — how width (`dtype_rate`) and complex structure (`complex_factor`) are priced, and why."},"1260":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-51","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Non-exploitability** — the invariants that keep billing sound, and the test that enforces each."},"1261":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-52","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Cost by family** — the rule + evidence + representative ops for the family you care about."},"1262":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-53","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Exhaustive per-op reference** — drill into `ops.json` for one op's exact formula."},"1263":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-54","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Completeness guarantee:** every billed operation is classified in the registry and\nappears in `ops.json` with a `cost_formula`; `tests/test_cost_model_coverage.py`\nenforces both that, and that every op-class (`ops.json` *area*) is covered by a family\nrule below. So nothing billed is undocumented, even where this doc summarizes by rule."},"1264":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-55","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"Every operation is charged `charged = int(flop_cost × dtype_rate × complex_factor × weight)`."},"1265":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-56","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**Four factors, on purpose.** Each factor isolates one kind of reasoning so it can be\nread off and audited on its own:"},"1266":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-57","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`flop_cost`** — the operation count. Every shape- and algorithm-dependent term lives\nhere, so anyone — us or a participant — can read it off the formula and audit it\nfunction by function."},"1267":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-58","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`dtype_rate`** — the width factor. One billed FLOP is one 32-bit-class real\noperation; a 64-bit-class operand bills `2×`. Like the weight ladder this is a *policy*\ntable (subject to tuning), not shape math — defined in\nDtype and precision."},"1268":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-59","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`complex_factor`** — the complex-structure factor. It is `1` for real dtypes and, for\ncomplex dtypes, the number of real (component-precision) operations one billed unit\nexpands into (a complex multiply is 6 real FLOPs, a complex add 2). It is *math*, not\npolicy — a textbook decomposition fixed per op, and computed per call for the\ncontraction family from the same accumulation that produces `flop_cost`."},"1269":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-60","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`weight`** — the hardware tier. A separate per-element factor for how much more one\nelement costs on real hardware: a plain sequential add or write is `1`, a\nnon-sequential memory access (a sort's comparison order, a computed-index gather, a\nrandom-reorder draw) is `4`, and a transcendental element (`sin`, `exp`, …) does many\ntimes more floating-point work at `16`. Rather than bill each op its exact measured\nratio — noisy, machine-specific, and hard to audit — we group operations into a small\nfixed ladder of tiers (`{0, 1, 4, 16}`); that grouping is a deliberate\ncompetition-design choice."},"1270":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-61","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"For a real 32-bit workload `dtype_rate` and `complex_factor` are both `1`, so the bill is\nexactly `int(flop_cost × weight)` and nothing here changes."},"1271":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-62","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"The rule that keeps the split honest — and the model non-gameable — is one **separation\ninvariant**: *a shape or algorithm constant never lives in a weight* (anything depending\non a matrix dimension or loop length belongs in `flop_cost`); &#x2A;width policy never lives in\n`flop_cost` or `weight`* (it lives in `dtype_rate`); and &#x2A;complex structure lives in\n`complex_factor`* — computed per call for the contraction family from the same\ndecomposition that produces `flop_cost`. (The weight half is enforced by\n`tests/test_weight_tier_policy.py`; the complex-classification half by\n`tests/test_complex_factor_completeness.py`.)"},"1272":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-63","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**We bill the textbook standard-algorithm cost, not literal BLAS/LAPACK.**\n`linalg.inv` is billed `2n³` (the standard LU-based `dgetrf`+`dgetri` operation\ncount) regardless of what the underlying library does; top-k SVD is billed as the\nstandard truncated-algorithm cost. This keeps billing deterministic,\nhardware-independent, and composable."},"1273":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-64","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"The rest of this section defines the conventions these principles rest on."},"1274":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-65","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fma2","content":"Each floating-point multiply, add, subtract, divide, or square root counts\nas 1 FLOP.  A fused multiply-add (FMA) therefore counts as 2.  This matches\nthe standard textbook convention.  All formulas in this document are stated\nin FMA=2 units unless noted."},"1275":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-66","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fma2","content":"All formulas count *operations*; the billing **unit** (one 32-bit-class real\nop) and the per-width and complex-structure pricing are defined once in\nDtype and precision."},"1276":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-67","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#comparison-and-select","content":"A single comparison (`>`, `==`, `!=`, …) or conditional-select (`where`,\n`choose`) counts as 1 FLOP.  Sorting, partition, and percentile operations\nuse this convention when counting per-element work."},"1277":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-68","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#transcendental-tier-weight-160","content":"Operations whose per-element cost is dominated by a libm minimax polynomial\nevaluation (sin, cos, tan, exp, log, arcsin, arccos, arctan, arcsinh,\narccosh, arctanh, power, and their NumPy 2.x aliases) are billed at weight\n16.0.  The `flop_cost` formula is `numel(output)` (1 per element); the 16×\nfactor is supplied entirely by the weight."},"1278":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-69","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#transcendental-tier-weight-160","content":"A subset of moderate-cost binary ops (floor\\_divide, mod/remainder, fmod,\narctan2, hypot, logaddexp, logaddexp2) is grouped into the same tier\n(weight 16.0)."},"1279":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-70","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"Operations whose per-element cost is dominated by a **non-sequential memory access** —\ncomparison-sort order, binary search, a computed-index gather, or a random-reorder draw\n— are billed at weight 4.0. `flop_cost` stays the plain algorithmic count (comparisons,\nindex dereferences, or draws); the 4× comes entirely from the weight, the same\none-factor-does-one-job split the transcendental tier uses."},"1280":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-71","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"This tier covers four families, each detailed in its own section below:"},"1281":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-72","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Sort / search** — `sort`, `argsort`, `lexsort`, `partition`, `argpartition`,\n`searchsorted` (see Sort and select)."},"1282":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-73","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Set / histogram** — `unique` and its NumPy 2.x siblings, `union1d`, `intersect1d`,\n`setdiff1d`, `setxor1d`, `histogram`, `histogram2d`, `histogramdd`,\n`histogram_bin_edges`, `bincount`, `digitize` (see Set ops and Interp and\nhistogram)."},"1283":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-74","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Gather** — `take`, `take_along_axis`, `choose`: a computed-index read is a\nnon-sequential memory access, unlike a materializing copy's sequential write (see\nCopy and gather)."},"1284":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-75","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Random reorder** — `random.permutation`, `random.shuffle`, `random.sample`, and the\n`Generator`/`RandomState` `.choice` / `.permutation` / `.permuted` / `.shuffle`\nmethods (see Random)."},"1285":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-76","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"A materializing copy or write (`concatenate`, `ones`, a scatter write) stays at weight\n1.0 — it touches memory sequentially, one write per element. The access tier prices the\n*pattern* of access, not merely the presence of a write."},"1286":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-77","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"> **flopscope meters both computation on values and the memory traffic that produces\n> them.** A **view** is free — it creates no new buffer and touches no element. Any op\n> that **writes a new buffer** is charged at least `1` per element written, whether the\n> values it writes are computed (`sin(x)`), copied (`concatenate`), replicated\n> (`tile`), or a repeated constant (`ones`). Ops whose access pattern is\n> **non-sequential** — sorting, a computed-index gather, a random-reorder draw — are\n> charged more per element (`4`) than a straight sequential write (`1`), because that\n> is where real hardware actually spends more per element: cache-friendly sequential\n> writes are cheap, branchy/random-order accesses are not."},"1287":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-78","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**The decision procedure** — apply these steps in order to any op:"},"1288":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-79","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**View / metadata only** (returns a view or read-only reinterpretation of existing\nmemory, inspects shape/dtype, allocates no new buffer)? → &#x2A;*Free (0).** Reshape,\nravel, and `copy` do **not** qualify even when NumPy happens to return a view —\nflopscope bills them as if they always materialize, so the price cannot depend on a\nlayout coincidence (see Views and metadata)."},"1289":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-80","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Does it write a new buffer sequentially** — a materializing copy (`concatenate`,\n`tile`, `roll`), a constant fill that isn't the zero-page default (`ones`, `full`,\n`eye`'s diagonal), a scatter write at a *given* index (`put`, `putmask`,\n`fill_diagonal`), or output-shaped selection (`select`, `compress`, `extract`)? →\n&#x2A;*Charged at weight 1.0.** `flop_cost` is the count of elements actually written\n(not the whole array when only part of it changes, e.g. `eye`'s off-diagonal zeros\nare free)."},"1290":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-81","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Does it read via a *non-sequential* access pattern** — a computed-index gather\n(`take`, `take_along_axis`, `choose`), a comparison-order derivation (`sort`,\n`unique`, `searchsorted`), a per-element conditional select (`where(cond, x, y)`,\nwhich dereferences a different source per output element and bills\n`4 × numel(broadcast output)`), or a random-reorder draw (`shuffle`, `permutation`,\n`choice`)? → **Charged at weight 4.0** (see Access tier)."},"1291":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-82","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Does it produce output values by doing floating-point arithmetic, *or* by\ncomparing element values?** → &#x2A;*Charged.** `flop_cost` = standard-algorithm op\ncount; `weight` = hardware tier. This includes elementwise math, transcendentals,\nreductions, contraction (matmul/einsum), FFT, polynomial, random generation, and\nops that *derive* a result by *testing values*: `nonzero`/`argwhere`/\n`flatnonzero`/`count_nonzero`/`where(1-arg)`, `clip`/`minimum`/`maximum`, and\n*computed creators* (`arange`/`linspace`/`geomspace`/`logspace`/`vander`)."},"1292":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-83","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Otherwise** — allocates a new buffer but writes nothing into it (`zeros`,\n`empty`) → &#x2A;*Free (0).** The OS zero-page (or uninitialized allocation) costs\nnothing to hand out; a participant only pays once real values land in it."},"1293":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-84","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Key invariant:** any predicate or index feeding a step-2/3 op was itself produced by\nan earlier charged step. A free-tier op may **never bundle** value-arithmetic,\nvalue-comparison, or a non-zero write into its own cost."},"1294":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-85","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"The active weights are `{0, 1, 4, 16}`. Weight 0 is now a narrow band — genuine views,\nmetadata, and all-zero/uninitialized allocation. Almost everything that writes a new\nbuffer bills at least weight 1.0; the historical \"free data movement\" framing (copy,\ngather, scatter, and select-with-a-given-selector all billed 0) has been replaced by\nthis write-metered model, since a participant who can move arbitrary amounts of data\nfor free can launder real compute through a materializing copy chain."},"1295":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-86","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"Weight 0 is now a narrow band — a new buffer is never allocated, or it is allocated\nbut nothing is written into it:"},"1296":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-87","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"**Views / metadata** — operations that return a view of existing memory or inspect\nmetadata without touching element values: `transpose`, `swapaxes`,\n`moveaxis`, `squeeze`, `expand_dims`, `flip`/`fliplr`/`flipud`, `rot90`,\n`atleast_1d`/`atleast_2d`/`atleast_3d`, `broadcast_to`, `asfortranarray`,\n`ascontiguousarray`, `view`, `real`/`imag` (component extraction), `split`,\n`hsplit`, `vsplit`, `array_split`, `unstack`, `diagonal` (the 2-D view path — see\nCopy-and-gather for the\n1-D *construct* path, which writes), `linalg.diagonal`, `linalg.matrix_transpose`,\n`from_dlpack` (zero-copy ingest), and all other shape/stride/dtype introspection\n(`ndim`, `shape`, `size`, `nbytes`, `itemsize`, `dtype`, `flags`, `base`, `data`,\n`ctypes`, `strides`, `T`, `isscalar`, `isfortran&#x60;).\n**`reshape`, `ravel`, `copy`, `flatten`, and `require` do *not* belong here** —\nflopscope bills them `numel(input)` at weight 1.0 unconditionally, even on the\n(common) call pattern where NumPy itself returns a view. Billing the cautious,\nalways-charged price avoids a participant relying on a layout coincidence\n(contiguity, stride pattern) to get a real copy for free. `flatten` is not even a\ncoincidence: `ndarray.flatten` is documented by NumPy to always allocate a new\nbuffer, never a view."},"1297":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-88","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"**All-zero / uninitialized allocation** — a new buffer whose contents are the\nplatform zero-page default, so nothing is actually written: `zeros`, `zeros_like`,\n`empty`, `empty_like`. Any *other* constant fill (`ones`, `full`, `eye`'s diagonal,\n`identity`, `tri`) writes real, non-zero values and is charged — see\nCopy and gather."},"1298":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-89","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"**Refinement — representation vs. value change** (resolves `astype`/`asarray`):"},"1299":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-90","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"> **A representation change that performs no work is free; every cast or copy that\n> actually runs is charged the same as `copy`.**"},"1300":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-91","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"`astype` and `asarray` bill `numel(input)` at the heavier of the source/destination\ndtype rate — the same formula `copy` bills (see Which dtype prices a\ncall) — for every call that does real work: narrowing,\nwidening, float→int truncation, bool coercion, a same-dtype `astype` called with the\ndefault `copy=True`, and any `asarray(x, dtype=other)` that actually converts the\nbuffer. The one free case left is the genuine no-op, where NumPy itself performs zero\nwork: `astype(x, dtype, copy=False)` when `dtype` already equals `x`'s dtype returns\nthe *identical object*, and `asarray(x)` with no `dtype=` (or a `dtype=` that already\nmatches `x`'s dtype) performs no conversion — both bill `0`, same as the views/metadata\nops above. Any other combination — `copy=False` across an actual dtype change (NumPy\ncannot honor the request and copies anyway), or any other `astype`/`asarray` call —\nperforms a real `numel`-element write and is charged."},"1301":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-92","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"This closes a loophole where `x.astype(x.dtype)` (default `copy=True`) was a free\nsubstitute for `x.copy()`, and `x.astype(bool)` / `x.astype(int)` were free substitutes\nfor a billed `!= 0` test or a `trunc`-then-cast. It also means choosing a narrower\nworking dtype now costs a one-time `copy`-priced toll to get there; the arithmetic\nperformed *afterward* on the narrower array is still cheaper per the dtype-rate table\n(see Which dtype prices a call), so that saving is not\neliminated — only no longer free to acquire up front. Contrast the charged cases with\n`ones`/`full`, which also touch every element but write an actual value into memory —\nthose are charged the same way (see Views and metadata\ncategory 2 above and Copy and gather)."},"1302":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-93","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"The method `a.nonzero()` is charged identically to `fnp.nonzero(a)`; `nonzero`,\n`argwhere`, `flatnonzero`, `count_nonzero`, and `where` (1-arg) remain charged\n`numel(input)` at weight 1.0 — they derive their result by testing element values\n(`!= 0`), and that test is real compute regardless of how \"small\" the eventual output\nis (see Copy and gather for the full selector-family accounting)."},"1303":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-94","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#composite-ops-weight-10-with-heterogeneous-flop_cost","content":"When an operation mixes sub-tiers internally (e.g. random samplers, stats\nkernels, norms with SVD), all per-element factors are folded into `flop_cost`\nand the active weight is set to 1.0.  This avoids double-counting with the\ntier factor."},"1304":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-95","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#numpy-2x-ufunc-aliases","content":"NumPy 2.x introduced `acos`, `acosh`, `asin`, `asinh`, `atan`, `atanh`,\n`atan2`, `pow`, and `divmod` as canonical aliases for their `arc*` /\n`power` / `floor_divide` counterparts (identical ufunc objects).  flopscope\nresolves these via `_UFUNC_ALIAS_RENAMES` in `_weights.py` so each alias\ncharges the same weight as its canonical twin."},"1305":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-96","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#dtype-and-precision","content":"`flop_cost` counts operations, but two operations of the same shape can do very different\namounts of real floating-point work depending on their **dtype**. Two factors price that:\n`dtype_rate` (how wide each scalar is) and `complex_factor` (how much a complex operation\nexpands into real ones). Both sit in the billing product\n`int(flop_cost × dtype_rate × complex_factor × weight)`, and both are `1.0` for the\n32-bit real baseline — so a real-fp32 workload bills exactly its `flop_cost × weight` and\nnothing in this section changes it."},"1306":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-97","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"We identified two ways a dtype-blind meter under-counts:"},"1307":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-98","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"**Complex packing.** A complex multiply does the work of four real multiplies and two\nreal adds, but a dtype-blind meter would bill `x * z` (with `z` complex) as one real\nmultiply. A participant can then carry two independent real payloads in the real and\nimaginary lanes of one complex array and recover both products from a single \"one-FLOP\"\nmultiply — buying two real results for less than the price of one. The same trick scales\nthrough every complex-valued op, most sharply through matmul, where one complex matmul\nstands in for two real ones."},"1308":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-99","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"**Width (bit) packing.** A float64 lane is twice as wide as a float32 lane. A dtype-blind\nmeter bills a float64 multiply the same as a float32 multiply, so a participant can pack\ntwo independent lower-precision payloads into the two halves of a 64-bit lane (or a narrow\ninteger into a wide one) and process both under a single op's charge."},"1309":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-100","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"The answer to both is the same, and it is **not** to ban wide or complex dtypes — it is\nto **price them**. `dtype_rate` charges a 64-bit-class op `2×` a 32-bit-class op;\n`complex_factor` charges a complex op the real-operation count it actually expands into.\nUnder that pricing neither pack pays off (see On packing)."},"1310":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-101","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"> **One billed FLOP is one 32-bit-class real scalar operation** — a float32 or int32 add,\n> multiply, compare, and so on."},"1311":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-102","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"Everything is priced relative to that anchor. `dtype_rate` is the width multiplier for the\ncall's resolved dtype:"},"1312":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-103","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"dtype"},"1313":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-104","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"rate"},"1314":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-105","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"dtype"},"1315":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-106","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"rate"},"1316":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-107","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`bool`"},"1317":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-108","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1318":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-109","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`int32`"},"1319":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-110","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1320":{"i