"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"4.0"},"1346":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-137","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex128`"},"1347":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-138","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"2.0"},"1348":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-139","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex192`"},"1349":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-140","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"3.0"},"1350":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-141","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex256`"},"1351":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-142","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"4.0"},"1352":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-143","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**32-bit-class and narrower → `1.0`.** float16/int16/int8/bool all bill at the baseline\nwidth."},"1353":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-144","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**64-bit-class → `2.0`.** float64, int64, uint64 do twice the scalar work of the\nbaseline width."},"1354":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-145","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**Extended precision → width class.** `float96` (3.0) and `float128` (4.0) are the\nplatform `longdouble` types (Linux only; on macOS/Windows `longdouble` aliases\n`float64`). At these rates, packing narrower payloads through their wider mantissas\nstill loses: two float32-payload products in one `float128` multiply cost `4.0`\nagainst the honest `2 × 1.0`. Note these dtypes cannot cross the evaluation wire\n(the transfer codec accepts the 14 standard dtypes) — they are priced for in-process\ncompute only."},"1355":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-146","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**complex → its component width's rate.** `complex64` is two float32 components → `1.0`;\n`complex128` is two float64 components → `2.0`; `complex192`/`complex256` follow their\nextended components (3.0 / 4.0). The *structure* cost of being complex (that a complex\nmultiply is several real ones) is carried separately by `complex_factor`, not folded\nin here."},"1356":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-147","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"These are **current policy values, subject to tuning** — the same status as the weight\nladder, and configured in the same file (`src/flopscope/data/default_weights.json`, under\n`\"dtype_rates\"`). They are a deliberate competition-design choice, not a hardware\nmeasurement."},"1357":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-148","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**Fail-closed for unknown numeric dtypes.** Any *numeric* dtype not in the table (a\nfuture type numpy or an extension package might introduce) has no defined rate, so\nbilling **raises `UnsupportedDtypeError` before charging any FLOPs** rather than\nguessing — a numeric dtype cannot slip through unpriced; supporting a new one is a\ndeliberate one-line policy addition to the table. *Non-numeric* dtypes (`object`,\n`str_`, `bytes_`, `datetime64`, `timedelta64`, structured/void) are not floating-point\narithmetic — no precision packing is possible through them — so they bill at the\nneutral rate `1.0`, preserving plain-numpy behavior for non-numeric arrays; their wall\ntime is covered by the residual-time penalty."},"1358":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-149","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`complex_factor` answers one question per op: &#x2A;when the resolved dtype is complex, how many\nreal (component-precision) FLOPs does one billed unit of this op actually perform?* It is\n`1.0` for real dtypes. For complex dtypes it is a fixed decomposition of the op into real\narithmetic (`z = a + bi`, FMA=2 convention):"},"1359":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-150","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"atom"},"1360":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-151","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"real FLOPs"},"1361":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-152","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"decomposition"},"1362":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-153","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"add / subtract / negate"},"1363":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-154","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"2"},"1364":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-155","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"two real adds"},"1365":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-156","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"multiply"},"1366":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-157","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"6"},"1367":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-158","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"4 real mul + 2 real add"},"1368":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-159","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"divide"},"1369":{"id"