t":"8 real FLOPs per FMA=2 unit (1 complex mul + 1 complex add)"},"1377":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-168","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"absolute"},"1378":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-169","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"4"},"1379":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-170","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"abs(z) = sqrt(re² + im²): 2 mul + 1 add + 1 sqrt"},"1380":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-171","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"sqrt"},"1381":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-172","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"10"},"1382":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-173","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"complex square root"},"1383":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-174","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"ordering compare"},"1384":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-175","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"2"},"1385":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-176","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"lexicographic: compare real parts, tie-break on imaginary"},"1386":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-177","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"Every charged op is classified by the atom its **billed unit** reduces to:"},"1387":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-178","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"class"},"1388":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-179","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"factor"},"1389":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-180","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"representative ops"},"1390":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-181","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"add / compare / sort / set / reduce-sum"},"1391":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-182","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**2**"},"1392":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-183","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"two real adds, two lexicographic compares, or two component relocations — a complex value is two real components, and every op prices at least one unit per component: `add`, `subtract`, `sum`, `mean`, `cumsum`, `sort`, `unique`, `where`, comparisons (`less`/`greater`/`equal`), set ops, `conj`, `angle`, `concatenate`, `take`, `random.shuffle`, and the wider copy/gather/creation family"},"1393":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-184","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"multiply / pure product"},"1394":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-185","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**6**"},"1395":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-186","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`multiply`, `prod` (`square` = 5)"},"1396":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-187","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"divide"},"1397":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-188","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**11**"},"1398":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-189","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`divide` (`reciprocal` = 6)"},"1399":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-190","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"variance family"},"1400":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-191","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**2.5**"},"1401":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-192","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`var`, `std` (square-and-sum: mostly adds, some multiplies)"},"1402":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-193","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"absolute / magnitude"},"1403":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-194","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**4**"},"1404":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-195","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`abs` (`hypot` itself is complex-illegal: numpy raises)"},"1405":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-196","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"transcendental"},"1406":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-197","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**per op**"},"1407":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-198","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`log` 2.25, `exp` 3.1, `sin`/`cos` 3.4, `tan` 3.6 — each from its complex closed form"},"1408":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-199","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"dense linalg"},"1409":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-200","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**4**"},"1410":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-201","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`inv`, `solve`, `svd`, `det`, `qr`, `eig`, … (complex factorization ≈ 4× the real arithmetic)"},"1411":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-202","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"contraction (FMA)"},"1412":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-203","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**exact**"},"1413":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-204","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`einsum`, `matmul`, `dot`, `inner`, `tensordot`, `vdot`, … — computed per call"},"1414":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-205","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"FFT"},"1415":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-206","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**1**"},"1416":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-207","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"no separate factor — the FFT formulas (`5N·log₂N`) already count the complex real-FLOPs: `fft.fft`, `fft.ifft`, `fft.rfft`"},"1417":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-208","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"complex-illegal"},"1418":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-209","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**raises**"},"1419":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-210","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`floor`/`ceil`/`trunc`, bitwise & shift ops, `mod`/`fmod`/`floor_divide`, real-only math (`cbrt`, `i0`, `unwrap`, …) and the real-only random samplers — anything numpy rejects on complex input"},"1420":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-211","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"Composite `counted_custom` ops carry a per-op derived factor of the same kind (for example\n`polyval` 4, `cross` 4.7, `interp` 3, `cov`/`corrcoef` 4). Two subtleties are worth\nstating explicitly."},"1421":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-212","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**Contraction is billed exactly, not with a flat factor.** A length-`K` dot product is `K`\nmultiplies + `K − 1` adds (real `flop_cost = 2K − 1`). In complex arithmetic that is `K`\ncomplex multiplies + `K − 1` complex adds = `6K + 2(K − 1) = 8K − 2` real FLOPs. The ratio\n`(8K − 2) / (2K − 1)` is `6` at `K = 1`, `≈ 4.13` at `K = 8`, and approaches `4` as\n`K → ∞` — it is **not** a constant. So the contraction family carries the sentinel\n`\"exact\"`, and the engine computes the complex total per call directly from the same\naccumulation that produced `flop_cost`: `6·(multiplies) + 2·(adds)`, with the multiply and\nadd counts read straight off the einsum cost object. A single flat factor would invite\n**alias-shopping** — route a multiply-heavy computation through the contraction whose flat\nfactor is cheapest. Exact per-call billing removes the arbitrage: `einsum('i,i->i', z, z)`\n(a pure elementwise product, `K = 1`, all multiplies) bills factor 6, identical to\n`multiply(z, z)`, not a discounted 4."},"1422":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-213","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**FFT is priced in at factor 1.** The FFT `flop_cost` of `5N·log₂N` is *already* a count\nof real FLOPs, so its `complex_factor` is `1.0`; applying a second complex factor would\ndouble-charge. Derivation: a radix-2 FFT does `(N/2)·log₂N` butterflies; each butterfly is\none complex multiply (6 real FLOPs) + two complex adds (2 × 2 = 4 real FLOPs) = 10 real\nFLOPs; `10 × (N/2)·log₂N = 5N·log₂N`. The complex structure is baked into `flop_cost`, so\n`complex_factor` stays `1`."},"1423":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-214","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**Guaranteed coverage.** Every charged op carries an explicit `complex_factor` (a number,\n`\"exact\"`, or `\"illegal\"`); `tests/test_complex_factor_completeness.py` fails the build if\nany charged op is unclassified. An op with no classification is necessarily\nfree / movement / blacklisted and bills factor `2` — a complex value is two real\ncomponents, and relocating one still prices one unit per component even though no\narithmetic happens."},"1424":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-215","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"The billing dtype for a call is **`np.result_type` over every array operand plus any\nexplicit `dtype=` / `out=` request** — the dtype the computation actually runs in —\ndeclared at each op's deduct site. Python scalars follow NumPy's NEP 50 weak promotion, so\n`f32_array * 2.0` stays float32 (the scalar does not up-promote the array)."},"1425":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-216","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"Worked consequences:"},"1426":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-217","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**Mixed kinds promote up.** `matmul(int32_array, float32_array)` resolves to `float64`\n(numpy's promotion for that mix) and bills at the float64 rate — the same as a genuine\nfloat64 matmul. There is no discount for feeding narrow-looking operands."},"1427":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-218","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**An explicit `dtype=` forces the loop.** `multiply(f64, f64, dtype=float32)` casts\nboth operands to float32 on read and runs the float32 loop — billed at the float32\nrate it actually computes (`1000` for a 1000-element call), not the float64 operand\npromotion. `out=` alone does not narrow the loop: `multiply(f64, f64, out=float32_arr)`\nstill runs the float64 loop and only casts the result into `out`, so it stays billed\nat the float64 rate (`2000`). The reverse direction is priced the same way: a\n**wider** `out=` bills the wider rate too — `multiply(f32, f32, out=float64_arr)`\nwrites a genuine float64 buffer, so it bills `2000`, identical to\n`astype(f32_array, float64)`. Storing into a wider buffer is real materialization\nwork, and pricing it at that wider rate holds `out=`-casting at exact `astype`\nparity in both directions: a wider `out=` costs what the equivalent `astype` costs,\nso `out=` can never be used to buy a cheaper wide copy than `astype` would."},"1428":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-219","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**A destination NumPy would have allocated anyway is price-neutral.** The rule above\nprices a destination as a buffer the call materializes — but two kinds of destination\nare not stores of the computed value at all, and naming them must cost exactly what\nletting NumPy allocate them costs. A **multi-output** op can have an output whose dtype\nbelongs to the op signature rather than to its arithmetic: `frexp`'s second output is\nalways `int32`, whatever precision the mantissa runs at, so `frexp(a_f32, out=(m_f32,\ne_i32))` bills what `frexp(a_f32)` bills (`10000` for a 10,000-element call), not the\nfloat64 rate that `result_type(float32, float32, int32)` would have promoted to. An\n**index reduction**'s destination holds positions, not values: `argmin`/`argmax`/\n`nanargmin`/`nanargmax` return `intp` regardless of the input's precision, so\n`argmin(a_f32, out=intp_arr)` bills what `argmin(a_f32)` bills (`9999`). Widening past\nNumPy's own choice still widens the rate in both families — a float64 mantissa or an\nint64 exponent on `frexp` bills `20000`. Index reductions additionally constrain `out=`\nby **kind**: NumPy accepts any integer or boolean buffer at any width and refuses every\nfloat and complex one, and that refusal is decided before the reduction runs, so it\ncosts `0`."},"1429":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-220","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**Casting/converting bills like `copy`.** `astype` and `asarray` bill `numel(input)`\nat the heavier of source/destination rate — via `heavier_billing_dtype`, not\n`result_type`, which would over-promote a cross-kind cast such as `float32 → int32`\nto float64 — whenever they perform real work: narrowing, widening, a value-changing\ncast (to-bool, float→int, complex→real), a same-dtype `astype` with the default\n`copy=True`, or any `asarray(x, dtype=other)` that actually converts the buffer. The\none free case is the genuine no-op: `astype(x, dtype, copy=False)` when `dtype`\nalready matches `x`'s dtype returns the identical object, and a dtype-free or\ndtype-matching `asarray(x)` performs no conversion — both bill `0` (see\nViews and metadata for the full rule). `copyto`, the\nin-place write primitive, is priced one unit per element written (per selected\nelement under `where=`), same-dtype or not."},"1430":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-221","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**`real` / `imag` are free.** Extracting a component of a complex value is a\nview / constant-fill, not arithmetic — `flop_cost = 0`."},"1431":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-222","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**Dtype-neutral bookkeeping declares `dtypes=()`.** Ops that carry no value dtype at\nall (for example `einsum_path`) resolve to rate `1.0`, factor `1.0`, so the rate table\ncannot perturb them. Ops with a *fixed* output dtype but no array operand — window\nfunctions, `fft.fftfreq`, the random samplers — are **not** neutral: they declare\ntheir output dtype (numpy produces float64 for all of these), so their real float64\narithmetic bills at the float64 rate like everything else."},"1432":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-223","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"A reduction is billed at the dtype numpy actually accumulates in. The accumulator\nresolves in numpy's own order: an explicit `dtype=` argument (positional or keyword) if\ngiven — billed exactly as requested, wider or narrower, because that request *is* the\naccumulator numpy runs; else `out`'s dtype if an output array is given — this widens the\naccumulator when `out` is wider than the input, but a narrower `out` only casts the\nfinal store, so the loop keeps the input's own width; else the family default — integer\nand boolean inputs widen to the platform default integer for `sum`/`prod`/`cumsum`/\n`cumprod` (and their `nan`-prefixed variants), and to `float64` for `mean`/`var`/`std`,\nnever billed below the input's own rate. Both `numpy.trace` and `linalg.trace` widen the\nsame way `sum` does — a batched diagonal sum is still a sum — and the generic\n`ufunc.reduce` / `accumulate` / `reduceat` paths resolve their accumulator through the\nidentical rule: `np.add.reduce` is the same machinery `sum` runs on, so\n`np.add.reduce(int32_arr, dtype=int32)` bills exactly like `sum(int32_arr, dtype=int32)`."},"1433":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-224","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`reduceat`'s base cost follows numpy's own per-segment semantics rather than a flat\nper-element charge: for segment `i`, `indices[i] < indices[i+1]` reduces the elements\nbetween them (a length-`L` segment costs `L-1` applications, the same `n-1` convention\n`reduce` uses), while a non-monotonic pair (`indices[i] >= indices[i+1]`) is a plain\nelement copy with no arithmetic — the segment costs 0. The final segment always runs to\nthe end of the axis. That per-segment count is then billed at the accumulator dtype,\nwhich widens the same way `reduce` does regardless of where the segment boundaries fall:\napplied as a single whole-axis segment (`indices=[0]`) to a 1000-element `int32` input,\nthat's one `L=1000` segment, 999 applications — `np.add.reduceat` bills at the int64 rate\n(999 × 2.0 = **1998**), while `np.subtract.reduceat` on the same input has no such\naccumulator and keeps the int32 loop (999 × 1.0 = **999**). `ufunc.outer` is not itself an\naccumulating reduction, but an explicit `dtype=` on it resolves through the same\nrequest-is-the-loop rule: the dtype names the loop numpy actually runs, not a discount,\nso `np.multiply.outer(int32_arr, int32_arr, dtype=float64)` bills the float64 rate over\nthe full outer-product grid — double the default int32-loop total."},"1434":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-225","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"Worked examples (production rates, 1000-element input, `sum`):"},"1435":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-226","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(int32_data)` — implicit int64 accumulator → 999 × 2.0 = **1998**."},"1436":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-227","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(int32_data, dtype=int32)` — explicit 32-bit accumulation is real 32-bit work →\n999 × 1.0 = **999**."},"1437":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-228","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(float32_data, None, float64)` — a positional wide accumulator bills exactly like\nthe keyword spelling `sum(float32_data, dtype=float64)` → 999 × 2.0 = **1998**."},"1438":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-229","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(float64_data, dtype=float32)` — numpy genuinely accumulates in float32 (operands\ncast to float32 on read), so the bill follows that narrower loop →\n999 × 1.0 = **999**."},"1439":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-230","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`trace` follows the identical rule at its own, much smaller element count:\n`linalg.trace` on an `8×8` int32 matrix bills its 8-element diagonal sum at the int64\nrate (**16**); the same shape in float32 stays at float32 (**8** — floats never widen).\nThe explicit accumulator carries through the top-level wrapper too:\n`trace(int32_matrix, dtype=int32)` bills **8**, at int32 — the same rate as the input,\nbecause an explicit accumulator request is honored exactly."},"1440":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-231","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"Two spellings resolve differently because they compute differently. Pointwise `dtype=`\ncomputes narrow: operands are cast to the requested dtype before the elementwise loop\nruns, so the bill is narrow too. Pointwise `out=` computes at the operand-promoted width\nand only casts the result on the way into `out`, so the bill stays wide. Reductions\nfollow the same split one level up: `dtype=` sets the accumulator in both directions —\nnarrower or wider, exactly as requested; `out=` widens the accumulator when `out` is\nwider than the input, while a narrower `out` only casts the final store, so it never\nbills below the input's own rate."},"1441":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-232","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Outside the accumulator machinery above, several numpy kernels simply run their\narithmetic at a wider dtype than the input's own — the bill follows where the\ncomputation actually happens, not the input's label:"},"1442":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-233","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"family"},"1443":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-234","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"integer / bool inputs compute in"},"1444":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-235","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"float inputs"},"1445":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-236","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unary float-only ufuncs (`exp`, `sin`, `sqrt`, …)"},"1446":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-237","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"the same-size float (`int8`/`uint8`/`bool` → `float16`; `int16`/`uint16` → `float32`; `int32`/`int64`/`uint32`/`uint64` → `float64`)"},"1447":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-238","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1448":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-239","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"binary float-only ufuncs (`divide`, `arctan2`, `hypot`, …)"},"1449":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-240","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`"},"1450":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-241","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1451":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-242","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float_power`"},"1452":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-243","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64` minimum"},"1453":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-244","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64` minimum — `float32` has no narrower loop either; `complex64` still promotes to `complex128`"},"1454":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-245","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"FFT family"},"1455":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-246","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`complex128` (no size-mapping — even `int8` runs the `complex128` path)"},"1456":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-247","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float16`/`float32` → `complex64`; `float64` → `complex128`"},"1457":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-248","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"LAPACK-backed `linalg.*`"},"1458":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-249","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`"},"1459":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-250","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged — single-precision drivers stay single"},"1460":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-251","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"mean-shaped composites (`average`, `median`, `gradient`, `percentile`, `nanmedian`, `nanpercentile`, …)"},"1461":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-252","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`, regardless of the integer's own width (no size-mapping, unlike the unary-ufunc row above)"},"1462":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-253","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1463":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-254","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"always-float64 composites (`polyfit`, `polyint`, …)"},"1464":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-255","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`"},"1465":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-256","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64` — forced even from `float32`"},"1466":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-257","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`exp` prices its size-mapped loop directly: on a 1000-element input, `int8` and `int16`\nboth still bill the `1.0` rate (**16000** — float16 and float32 share the baseline rate),\nwhile `int32`/`int64` bill the `2.0` float64 rate (**32000**). `divide(int32, int32)`\nbills 2.0 per element (numpy true-divides in float64); `divide(float32, float32)` still\nbills 1.0 — the promotion only fires for the operand mixes numpy itself promotes, not a\nblanket float64 fold. `fft.fft` of a length-1024 (`N`) signal has a `5N·log₂N` = 51200\nreal-FLOP count before the dtype rate; an int32 signal bills that count at the\ncomplex128 rate (**102400**), while a float32 signal keeps the complex64 rate and bills\nthe raw **51200** unscaled."},"1467":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-258","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"A single non-inexact operand forces `linalg.*`'s promotion, regardless of how wide the\n*other* operand is: an `8×8` `linalg.solve(int32_matrix, int32_vector)` bills float64\n(**938**), and so does mixing kinds — `solve(bool_matrix, float32_vector)` on the same\nshape bills the identical **938**, exactly as if the matrix were genuinely float64. The\nall-float32 system keeps the single-precision driver and bills half that (**469**)."},"1468":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-259","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Integer contractions keep their input's own rate: `matmul`, `dot`, `einsum`, and\n`matrix_power` with a non-negative exponent genuinely run integer arithmetic\n(`matmul(int32, int32)` stays int32). `matrix_power` with a *negative* exponent is the\nexception — numpy inverts the matrix first, which routes through the same LAPACK\nfloat64 promotion as `linalg.inv`: a `4×4` `matrix_power(int32_matrix, -1)` bills\nfloat64 (**224**) while `matrix_power(int32_matrix, 2)` stays int32 (**112**), and\n`matrix_power(float32_matrix, -1)` keeps the single-precision driver (**112**) even\nwhile inverting."},"1469":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-260","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Not every widening composite forces float64 unconditionally, and not every one lands on\nfloat64 at all — the billed dtype is always whatever `resolved_dtype` records for that\nspecific call, not one fixed target per op family. `roots` builds a companion matrix and\nfinds its eigenvalues, so it inherits `linalg.eig`'s dtype rule directly: an integer\ninput computes float64, but a float32 input keeps its own single-precision driver.\n`poly` (rebuilding coefficients from roots) runs a different algorithm — iterative\nconvolution, not eigendecomposition — but resolves its dtype the same preserve-if-inexact\nway. Neither belongs in the always-float64 row above. `vander` promotes through\n`numpy.promote_types(x.dtype, int)`, the same kind of rule a reduction accumulator uses:\na narrow integer lands on the platform `int64` (`vander(int32_x)` bills its int64 rate,\nnot float64), while float and complex inputs still land on `float64` / `complex128`."},"1470":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-261","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"The generic `ufunc.outer` and `ufunc.at` methods apply this same per-element promotion\nto their base ufunc, so there is no method-shaped way around it:\n`np.hypot.outer(int32_arr, int32_arr)` bills the float64 rate over its full\nouter-product grid, and `np.exp.at(int32_arr, ...)` bills the float64 rate over the\nindices it touches — identical to calling `hypot` or `exp` directly."},"1471":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-262","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Zero-work contractions charge **0** regardless of dtype. `einsum('ij->ji', z)` is a pure\ntranspose — no multiply-adds happen — and a `matmul` with an empty operand has no terms\nto sum, so both bill 0 even when `z` is complex128; a same-shape *non-empty* complex128\nmatmul still bills its exact contraction total (see Contraction is billed\nexactly)."},"1472":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-263","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"**Guaranteed coverage.** `tests/test_compute_dtype_conformance.py` probes every charged\nregistry op with a discriminating int32 input (or records why it is exempt) and asserts\nthe billed rate is at least the rate of both the NEP-50-promoted input dtype and numpy's\nactual result dtype — an op that quietly starts computing in a wider dtype than it bills\nfails the build instead of shipping an undercount. The sweep covers charged *registry*\nops; the dynamic ufunc-method surface (`.outer`, `.reduceat`, `.at`, `.reduce`,\n`.accumulate`, which bill under per-call op names like `hypot.outer` rather than registry\nkeys) is out of its reach and is locked by targeted tests in `tests/test_dtype_cost.py`\ninstead, including the same input-rate floor."},"1473":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-264","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Reproduce any of these yourself: run the call inside a `BudgetContext` and read\n`op_log[-1].resolved_dtype` alongside `flops_used`."},"1474":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-265","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"Billed FLOPs for an `N = 100` elementwise call under production rates (`multiply`: weight\n1, factor 6; `add`: weight 1, factor 2; `sin`: weight 16, factor 3.4):"},"1475":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-266","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"op"},"1476":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-267","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"float32"},"1477":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-268","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"float64"},"1478":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-269","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"complex64"},"1479":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-270","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"complex128"},"1480":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-271","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"`multiply`"},"1481":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-272","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"100"},"1482":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-273","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"200"},"1483":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-274","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"600"},"1484":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-275","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"1200"},"1485":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-276","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"`add`"},"1486":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-277","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"100"},"1487":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-278","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"200"},"1488":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-279","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"200"},"1489":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-280","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"400"},"1490":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-281","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"`sin`"},"1491":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-282","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"1600"},"1492":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-283","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"3200"},"1493":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-284","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"5440"},"1494":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-285","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"10880"},"1495":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-286","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"Reading one cell: `multiply` on `complex128` = `int(100 × 2.0 × 6.0 × 1.0) = 1200` —\n`flop_cost` 100, float64-component rate 2.0, complex-multiply factor 6.0, baseline weight\n1.0. `add` on `complex64` = `int(100 × 1.0 × 2.0 × 1.0) = 200`: a complex add's factor is\nonly 2, so it costs far less than the complex multiply. `sin` carries its weight-16\ntranscendental tier and a 3.4 complex factor, so `complex128` =\n`int(100 × 2.0 × 3.4 × 16) = 10880`."},"1496":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-287","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"Contraction is exact rather than a flat factor: a `matmul` of two `(8, 8)` matrices bills\n`960` real (`2·8³ − 8²`), `1920` at float64, and `3968` at complex64 — the engine's exact\n`6·512 + 2·448` (512 scalar multiplies, 448 adds), a ratio of `≈ 4.13`, not `4`. At\ncomplex128 it is `7936` (that exact total × the 2.0 rate). These values are pinned in\n`tests/test_dtype_cost.py`."},"1497":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-288","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"Packing is **not banned and not judged** — it is priced so it does not pay:"},"1498":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-289","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Complex packing loses.** Two honest real multiplies of 100-element float32 arrays bill\n`200`; folding the two payloads into one complex64 multiply bills `600` (factor 6) — a\n`3×` loss. At matmul scale the packed complex matmul bills `3968` against the `1920` of\nthe two honest real matmuls — roughly `2×` worse, and that is before any pack/unpack\noverhead."},"1499":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-290","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Width packing breaks even at best.** Two float32 multiplies bill `2 × 100 = 200`; one\nfloat64 multiply carrying both payloads bills `100 × 2.0 = 200` — identical, and that is\n*before* the arithmetic to pack the lanes in and unpack the results out, which is itself\ncharged. So 32-into-64 packing is break-even-or-losing."},"1500":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-291","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Sub-32-bit lane tricks** (packing several int8 or int16 payloads into a wider lane) can\nin principle recover a small constant-factor advantage, since everything at or below\n32-bit shares the `1.0` rate. That gain is bounded and small, is considered in-bounds,\nand the rate table deliberately does not chase it."},"1501":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-292","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"Billing follows the loop numpy actually runs, in both directions: an explicit narrow\n`dtype=` — pointwise, or a reduction's accumulator — bills narrow because the arithmetic\ngenuinely happens at that width, trading precision for a cheaper bill exactly as\nrequested. `out=` alone never narrows a loop, so it cannot be used to buy a narrow bill\nfor wide compute. The width-rate and complex-factor pricing above is what makes packing\na loss or a break-even; there is no separate dtype-request rule needed to close it."},"1502":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-293","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"The cost model meters compute so a participant cannot do expensive real work while\nbeing billed cheaply. The two threats are **under-count** (an op billed below its\ntrue cost) and **substitution arbitrage** (routing the same work through a\ncheaper-billed but equivalent op). The model defends against both with invariants,\neach backed by a CI-enforced test you can open and read:"},"1503":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-294","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"Invariant"},"1504":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-295","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"What it guarantees"},"1505":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-296","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"Enforced by"},"1506":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-297","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Faithful cost**"},"1507":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-298","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"each `flop_cost` is the real standard-algorithm op count, with every shape/algorithm constant inside `flop_cost`"},"1508":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-299","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"per-op evidence in §Cost by family; `test_cost_constant_unification.py`, `test_cost_formula_vs_code.py`"},"1509":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-300","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Weight-tier policy**"},"1510":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-301","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"every active weight ∈ `{0, 1, 4, 16}`; arithmetic ops are 0, 1, or 4; **no algorithm constant in a weight**"},"1511":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-302","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_weight_tier_policy.py`"},"1512":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-303","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No substitution arbitrage**"},"1513":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-304","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a bit-identical alias cannot bill cheaper than its canonical (e.g. `acos` *is* `arccos` — the 16× ufunc-alias fix); equivalent contractions (`dot`/`inner`/`matmul`/`einsum`) share one cost engine"},"1514":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-305","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_ufunc_alias_parity.py`, `test_random_weight_aliasing.py`; the shared einsum engine (§Contraction)"},"1515":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-306","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No cheap in-op path**"},"1516":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-307","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"top-k `svd(k=)` cannot yield a *full* decomposition below full price (the `min(4mnk, economy)` cap + `k ≥ min → full` guard); invalid `k` (`< 1` or `> min(m, n)`) is rejected before any billing"},"1517":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-308","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_svd_topk_cost.py` (cap / guard / monotonicity); `test_linalg.py` (invalid-`k` `ValueError`)"},"1518":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-309","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Free-tier discipline**"},"1519":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-310","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"weight 0 is limited to views/metadata, untouched (zero-page or uninitialized) allocation, and the narrow `astype`/`asarray` no-op (`copy=False` with an already-matching dtype; a dtype-free or dtype-matching `asarray`) — every other cast or copy, including a same-dtype `astype(copy=True)`, bills `numel` like `copy`. Any op that writes a new buffer — copied, replicated, constant-filled, gathered, or scattered — carries weight ≥ 1. Every value-test is charged wherever it hides: `a.nonzero()` (method), `where(1-arg)`, `argwhere`, `flatnonzero`, `count_nonzero`"},"1520":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-311","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_weight_tier_policy.py`; `test_data_movement_free_tier.py` (free-labels consistency guard)"},"1521":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-312","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No free-gather discount**"},"1522":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-313","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a computed-index gather (`take`, `take_along_axis`, `choose`) is metered at the access tier (weight 4.0) like any other non-sequential read, so precomputing a look-up table and then gathering from it no longer buys a categorical discount; only genuine view-indexing (a static/basic index, `arr[i]`) stays free"},"1523":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-314","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_data_movement_free_tier.py`; §Copy and gather"},"1524":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-315","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Complex packing non-profitable**"},"1525":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-316","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"folding two real payloads into one complex op's real/imag lanes bills the op's true complex structure (`multiply` factor 6, matmul exact `≈4.13×`), so the pack costs more than the honest real work it replaces"},"1526":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-317","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`tests/test_dtype_cost.py` (packing tests)"},"1527":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-318","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Width packing break-even-or-losing**"},"1528":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-319","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a 64-bit op bills `2×` a 32-bit op (`dtype_rate`), so packing two 32-bit payloads into one 64-bit lane is break-even before pack/unpack overhead; billing follows the loop numpy actually runs, so an explicit narrow `dtype=` only bills narrow when the compute is genuinely that narrow, and `out=` alone never shrinks the loop"},"1529":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-320","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`tests/test_dtype_cost.py` (width-packing tests)"},"1530":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-321","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**End-to-end billing**"},"1531":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-322","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"production billing is pinned per weight tier `{0,1,4,16}` (catches a silent weight regression); the retired `8.0` tier is documented as retired rather than silently dropped"},"1532":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-323","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_production_weight_billing.py`"},"1533":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-324","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"An auditor can read this table top-to-bottom and, for each claim, open the named test\nto see exactly what guarantees it. The first two rows are the load-bearing ones: an exact\n`flop_cost` defeats under-count, and the weight-tier policy (no constant in a weight)\ndefeats the family of arbitrage exploits where a high-constant op is re-tiered cheaply."},"1534":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-325","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-by-family","content":"Each family below is one **rule** + its **evidence/citation** + **representative ops**.\nThe rule is the part to audit; the per-op tables are kept where each op carries a\n*distinct* cited constant (linalg, FFT, polynomial, stats, window, random) because\nthose constants are the evidence — and because `ops.json`'s generated `cost_formula`\nis coarse for many composite ops (it records `per-operation` where the real formula is\nshape-dependent). For families whose members all share one rule (copy/gather, views),\nonly representatives are listed and the full set is a filter in\n`ops.json`."},"1535":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-326","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Family rule**: `flop_cost = numel(output)`."},"1536":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-327","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Baseline tier (weight 1.0)**: arithmetic (+, −, ×, ÷, √), rounding\n(ceil, floor, trunc, rint, around/round), sign/abs, logical (not, and, or,\nxor), bitwise (and, or, xor, invert, left\\_shift, right\\_shift), comparisons\n(equal, not\\_equal, greater, less, greater\\_equal, less\\_equal), copies\n(positive, negative, conj/conjugate, fabs, modf, frexp, spacing,\nnan\\_to\\_num, isclose, isneginf, isposinf, deg2rad/degrees, rad2deg/radians,\nldexp, nextafter, copysign, heaviside, signbit), and their NumPy aliases.\n(`real`/`imag` are **not** here — component extraction is free; see\nView / free.)"},"1537":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-328","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Transcendental tier (weight 16.0)**: exp, exp2, expm1, log, log2, log10,\nlog1p, cbrt, sin, cos, tan, sinh, cosh, tanh, arcsin, arccos, arctan,\narcsinh, arccosh, arctanh, sinc, i0, power, angle, and their NumPy 2.x\naliases (asin, acos, atan, asinh, acosh, atanh, atan2, pow)."},"1538":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-329","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Moderate binary tier (weight 16.0)**: arctan2/atan2, hypot, logaddexp,\nlogaddexp2, floor\\_divide, mod/remainder, fmod, float\\_power. `float_power` always\nreturns float64 — numpy's own promotion rule for that ufunc — so it bills at the\nfloat64 rate even when both operands are float32."},"1539":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-330","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Basis**: DECLARED per-element FMA=2 convention and empirical calibration."},"1540":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-331","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Complex dtypes**: each op bills its per-op `complex_factor` from the class table in\nDtype and precision — `add`/comparisons 2,\n`multiply` 6, `divide` 11, `abs` 4, transcendentals per op (`sin` 3.4, `log` 2.25); `conj`\nand `angle` price the same component floor (factor 2); rounding, bitwise, and\n`mod`-family ops are complex-illegal and raise."},"1541":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-332","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_pointwise.py`."},"1542":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-333","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"**Family rule**: `flop_cost = numel(input) − numel(output)` (orbit-mapping\nmodel; one add or compare per element consumed by the reduction)."},"1543":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-334","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"Ops that do more than one accumulation pass carry the extra passes in\n`flop_cost` (never in the weight column): the variance family makes four\npasses (mean-sum, centre, square, variance-sum), `ptp` makes two (max + min)\nplus the per-output subtract, and `mean`/`average` add the per-output divide."},"1544":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-335","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"Op"},"1545":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-336","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1546":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-337","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"weight"},"1547":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-338","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"basis"},"1548":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-339","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`sum`, `prod`, `max`, `min`, `any`, `all`, `nansum`, `nanmax`, `nanmin`, `nanprod`"},"1549":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-340","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input) − numel(output)"},"1550":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-341","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1551":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-342","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED reduction skeleton (one add or compare per consumed element)"},"1552":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-343","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`cumsum`, `cumprod`, `nancumsum`, `nancumprod`, `cumulative_sum`, `cumulative_prod`"},"1553":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-344","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input) − num\\_output\\_slices (= n−1 for a full 1-D scan; product of non-reduced dims otherwise)"},"1554":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-345","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1555":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-346","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED: scan accumulation; output shape = input shape so the generic `numel(in)−numel(out)` formula evaluates to 0 — these use the correct per-slice count instead"},"1556":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-347","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`mean`, `average` (unweighted)"},"1557":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-348","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input)"},"1558":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-349","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1559":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-350","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED: reduction (numel−M) + M divides"},"1560":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-351","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`average(weights=)`"},"1561":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-352","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`3·numel − M`, M = num output slices (1 for full reduction)"},"1562":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-353","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1563":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-354","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED: a·w multiply pass (numel) + a·w sum (numel−M) + weight sum (numel−M) + M divides"},"1564":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-355","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`std`, `var`, `nanstd`, `nanvar`"},"1565":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-356","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"≈ 4 × numel(input) (std: + M sqrt)"},"1566":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-357","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1567":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-358","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED four-pass: mean-sum, centre, square, var-sum (exact: 2·numel + 2·(numel−M) + 2M)"},"1568":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-359","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`argmax`, `argmin`"},"1569":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-360","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input) − num\\_output\\_slices (= n−1 for full 1-D; reduction\\_cost model)"},"1570":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-361","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1571":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-362","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED scan: same orbit model as reduction family"},"1572":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-363","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`median`, `nanmedian`"},"1573":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-364","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"axis length per output slice"},"1574":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-365","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1575":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-366","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED; partition (introselect) per output"},"1576":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-367","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`percentile`, `nanpercentile`, `quantile`, `nanquantile`"},"1577":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-368","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"per output slice, piecewise in axis length `n` and `q.size` `k` — unweighted: `n·min(k, 1 + 4⌈log₂ min(k,n)⌉) + 4k`; with `weights=`: `4n⌈log₂ n⌉ + 3n + k(⌈log₂ n⌉ + 4)`"},"1578":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-369","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1579":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-370","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED; unweighted bills the cheaper of `k` partition passes or one shared sort-parity pass (a dense `q` returns the sorted input); the weighted branch sorts internally, so it is priced at sort parity plus a per-`q` lookup"},"1580":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-371","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`ptp`"},"1581":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-372","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"2 × numel(input) − numel(output)"},"1582":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-373","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1583":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-374","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED: max pass + min pass + M subtracts (2·(numel−M)+M)"},"1584":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-375","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`count_nonzero`"},"1585":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-376","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input)"},"1586":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-377","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1587":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-378","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED comparison scan (every element tested regardless of axis)"},"1588":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-379","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`nanmean`"},"1589":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-380","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input)"},"1590":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-381","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1591":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-382","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED: reduction (numel−M) + M divides; billed identically to mean"},"1592":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-383","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"**Complex dtypes**: sum-type reductions (`sum`, `mean`, `cumsum`, `nansum`, …) bill factor\n2 (complex add); product reductions (`prod`, `cumprod`, `nanprod`) factor 6 (complex\nmultiply); the variance family (`var`, `std`, `nanvar`, `nanstd`) factor 2.5."},"1593":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-384","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_pointwise.py`; reduction accumulation model in\n`src/flopscope/_accumulation/`."},"1594":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-385","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Every op in this family is billed by **one shared, symmetry-aware engine**\n(`_resolve_cost_and_output_symmetry` → `einsum_cost`); the closed forms below are\nthat engine's output specialised to each op's shapes, not separately maintained\nconstants."},"1595":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-386","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Family rule:**"},"1596":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-387","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(2K − 1)` is one length-`K` dot product: `K` multiplies + `K − 1` adds (FMA=2)."},"1597":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-388","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`K` = product of the **contracted** (summed) axis dimensions."},"1598":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-389","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`M` = number of output cells the engine computes. This is `prod(output dims)`\nfor a generic contraction, but the engine **reduces it to the unique-orbit\ncount when it can prove the output is symmetric** — when operands alias the\nsame array (`outer(v, v)`, `inner(A, A)`) or carry an `as_symmetric` tag. It\nnever invents savings: `A @ A` for a general `A` still costs the full\n`2n³ − n²`, because `A @ A` is not symmetric."},"1599":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-390","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Op"},"1600":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-391","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/fl