odel#contraction-einsum-family","content":"symmetric `M`"},"1633":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-424","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1634":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-425","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`outer(v, v)`"},"1635":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-426","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n²`"},"1636":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-427","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n(n+1)/2`"},"1637":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-428","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n(n+1)/2`"},"1638":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-429","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`inner(A, A)`"},"1639":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-430","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n²`"},"1640":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-431","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n(n+1)/2`"},"1641":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-432","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(2n − 1) · n(n+1)/2`"},"1642":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-433","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`einsum` runs the accumulation directly as `(K − 1)·M + α`, where `α` is the\nnumber of unique (output + contracted) index combinations — equal to `K·M` for a\nsingle clean contraction, but more general for multi-index or broadcast\nsubscripts. A multi-operand einsum (`≥ 3` operands) walks the `opt_einsum`\noptimal binary path and sums per-step costs. Batched/stacked variants of any row\nabove multiply the closed form by the batch size."},"1643":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-434","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Compound linalg** ops are *chains* of matmuls, billed as the sum of their steps\nthrough the `matmul_cost(m, k, n)` helper — which itself delegates to\n`einsum_cost('ij,jk->ik', …)`, so each step equals a 2-D matmul by construction\n(no duplicated `2mkn − mn` constant to drift). `linalg.pinv` and `linalg.lstsq`\nbuild on the same helper."},"1644":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-435","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Op"},"1645":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-436","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1646":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-437","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"basis"},"1647":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-438","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`linalg.matrix_power`"},"1648":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-439","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(⌊log₂ k⌋ + popcount(k) − 1) × matmul_cost(n, n, n)`"},"1649":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-440","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"repeated squaring"},"1650":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-441","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`linalg.multi_dot`"},"1651":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-442","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"sum of optimal-chain matmul costs; each step `2mkn − mn`"},"1652":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-443","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"optimal chain order"},"1653":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-444","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Complex dtypes**: the contraction family is billed **exactly**, not with a flat factor.\nThe engine expands each call's `flop_cost` into `6·(multiplies) + 2·(adds)` from the same\naccumulation decomposition (see\nthe exact-contraction rule). For a length-`K`\ndot product the complex/real ratio is `(8K − 2)/(2K − 1)` (6 at `K=1`, `≈4.13` at `K=8`),\nso `einsum('i,i->i', z, z)` bills exactly like a complex `multiply`."},"1654":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-445","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"All contraction ops use **weight 1.0** — the shape formulas already carry the\nfull FMA=2 cost. Source: `_pointwise.py` (op wrappers), `_einsum.py`\n(`_resolve_cost_and_output_symmetry`), `_flops.py` (`einsum_cost`,\n`matmul_cost`), `_accumulation/` (accumulation model)."},"1655":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-446","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"Op"},"1656":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-447","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1657":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-448","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"basis"},"1658":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-449","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"source"},"1659":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-450","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"`arange`"},"1660":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-451","page_id":"/docs/u