-eigen--svd","content":"`2ab² + 2b³`"},"1865":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-656","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"DERIVED: SVD values only, no vectors (dgesdd)"},"1866":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-657","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"`_decompositions.py:svdvals_cost`"},"1867":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-658","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"`roots`"},"1868":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-659","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"`10n³`, `n` = stripped companion dimension (leading and trailing zero coefficients removed before companion matrix is built)"},"1869":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-660","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"DERIVED: companion-matrix eigvals (delegates to eigvals\\_cost on trimmed degree)"},"1870":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-661","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"`_polynomial.py`; consistent with polynomial-table `roots` row"},"1871":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-662","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"`linalg.svd(..., k=)` and `linalg.svdvals(..., k=)` accept a top-k parameter.\nFor `1 ≤ k < min(m, n)` the billed cost is"},"1872":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-663","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"min(4·m·n·k, economy)"},"1873":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-664","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"where `economy` is the full thin/values-only cost above. `4·m·n·k` is the\nleading-order cost (FMA=2, Θ(mnk)) of a rank-k truncated SVD (two\nunavoidable passes over A). It is billed as the\n**standard truncated-algorithm cost of the operation** — consistent with how\nthis model bills direct-linalg ops at their textbook standard-algorithm count\nrather than literal BLAS/LAPACK work — even though the reference implementation\ncomputes the full economy SVD\nand slices (results stay exact). Unlike the full case, **values-only is not\nleading-order cheaper** for top-k. `k = min(m, n)` (all components) bills the\nfull economy cost, and the `full_matrices` full-U premium applies only to the\nfull decomposition (`k is None`); so a complete decomposition can never be\nobtained below full price. Invalid `k` (`< 1` or `> min(m, n)`) raises\n`ValueError`."},"1874":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-665","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"**Accepted residual:** because `4mnk < 6ab²+20b³` for all `k ≤ min(m, n)`, the\ntruncated rate applies up to `k = min(m, n) − 1`, so a caller can obtain up to\n`min(m, n) − 1` exact singular vectors at the truncated rate. The guard ensures\nthey can never obtain **all** `min(m, n)` components below full price."},"1875":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-666","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"Per-matrix cost is multiplied by the batch dimension product.  Constants\nmarked \"provisional\": iteration counts are input-dependent and the cubic\nconstant is the standard textbook estimate."},"1876":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-667","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"**Family rule** (DERIVED, radix-2 FFT — 5 real ops per butterfly):"},"1877":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-668","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"Op"},"1878":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-669","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1879":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-670","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"basis"},"1880":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-671","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fft`, `fft.ifft`"},"1881":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-672","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × N × ⌈log₂ N⌉`, `N` = transform length"},"1882":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-673","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: 5 real ops per butterfly"},"1883":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-674","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fft2`, `fft.ifft2`, `fft.fftn`, `fft.ifftn`"},"1884":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-675","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"staged: `Σ` of the per-axis 1-D `fft` costs over numpy's execution cascade (reverse axis order), each `5 × batchᵢ × dᵢ × ⌈log₂ dᵢ⌉` at that axis's *current* intermediate shape; reduces to `5 × N × Σᵢ⌈log₂ dᵢ⌉`, `N = prod(transform dims)`, when `s` resizes no axis"},"1885":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-676","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: sum of the 1-D cascade; the final-shape product holds only when no axis is resized by `s`"},"1886":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-677","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.rfft`, `fft.irfft`"},"1887":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-678","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × (N/2) × ⌈log₂ N⌉`"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-679","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: real-input / real-output half-spectrum"},"1889":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-680","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.rfft2`, `fft.irfft2`, `fft.rfftn`, `fft.irfftn`"},"1890":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-681","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"staged: a real 1-D FFT on the last axis (`5 × (d_last/2) × ⌈log₂ d_last⌉`) plus complex 1-D FFTs on the remaining axes over the half-spectrum intermediate, summed in numpy's order (r2c: real axis first; c2r: real axis last)"},"1891":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-682","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: per-stage cascade; the half-spectrum shrinks the downstream batch, so it differs from `5 × (N/2) × Σᵢ⌈log₂ dᵢ⌉` except in the no-resize two-axis case"},"1892":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-683","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.hfft`"},"1893":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-684","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × (n_out/2) × ⌈log₂ n_out⌉`"},"1894":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-685","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: hfft = irfft(conj(a)) — conjugate-symmetry halves the work"},"1895":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-686","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.ihfft`"},"1896":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-687","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × (n/2) × ⌈log₂ n⌉`"},"1897":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-688","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: same `hfft_cost(n)` formula"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-689","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fftfreq`"},"1899":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-690","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`n` (index grid scaled by `1/(n*d)` — one divide per output element)"},"1900":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-691","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DECLARED: `n` divides"},"1901":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-692","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.rfftfreq`"},"1902":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-693","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`n//2 + 1` (real-spectrum grid has `n//2 + 1` elements)"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-694","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DECLARED: `n//2 + 1` divides"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-695","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fftshift`, `fft.ifftshift`"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-696","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`numel(output)`"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-697","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: `numpy.roll`-based data-movement reindex, billed at weight 1.0 like a materializing copy (not part of the FFT priced-in family) — see Copy and gather"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-698","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"The table above gives the **per-transform** cost; every transform op also multiplies by\nhowever many independent transforms the call performs. `fft`/`ifft`/`rfft`/`irfft`\ntransform a single `axis` and batch over every other axis (`numel(input) / N`).\n`fft2`/`ifft2`/`rfft2`/`irfft2`/`fftn`/`ifftn`/`rfftn`/`irfftn` transform the given\n`axes` — or, when `axes` is omitted but `s` is given, the trailing `len(s)` axes, not\nevery axis — and batch over whatever axes remain."},"1908":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-699","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"**Complex dtypes**: `complex_factor` is **1** (priced in) — `5N·log₂N` already counts the\ncomplex transform's real FLOPs (10 per radix-2 butterfly), so a complex128 transform bills\nonly the `2.0` dtype rate over its float32 counterpart, with no extra structural factor\n(see FFT is priced in)."},"1909":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-700","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"All counted FFT ops use **weight 1.0**.  Source: `src/flopscope/numpy/fft/_transforms.py`."},"1910":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-701","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"Op"},"1911":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-702","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-703","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"basis"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-704","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"source"},"1914":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-705","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyval`"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-706","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`2 × deg × points` (Horner: 1 mul + 1 add per coefficient per point, FMA=2)"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-707","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-708","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py`"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-709","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyfit`"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-710","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`m×deg` (Vandermonde build) `+ lstsq_cost(m, deg+1, ncols)` (`ncols = y.shape[1]` for 2-D `y`, else 1)"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-711","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED: builds an `(m, deg+1)` Vandermonde matrix and solves it via `lstsq` (SVD least-squares) — prices the identical solve `linalg.lstsq` charges for the same shape, rather than a cheaper standalone normal-equations estimate; the SVD factorization is shared across `ncols` RHS columns, so cost grows sublinearly in `ncols`, not linearly"},"1921":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-712","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polyfit_cost`"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-713","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyadd`, `polysub`"},"1923":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-714","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"size of the axis-0 zero-padded, broadcast-aligned result (= `numpy.polyadd(a1, a2).size`; reduces to `max(n1, n2, 1)` for 1-D inputs)"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-715","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED: mirrors numpy's own zero-pad-then-broadcast algorithm on shapes, including higher-rank/broadcasting operands"},"1925":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-716","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py`"},"1926":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-717","p