odel#polynomial","content":"`poly` (1-D, build from roots)"},"1935":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-726","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`(3n² + n) // 2`, `n = len(roots)` (iterative convolution with length-2 kernel per root; FMA=2)"},"1936":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-727","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"1937":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-728","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:poly_cost`"},"1938":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-729","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyder`"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-730","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`t × n − t(t+1)/2`, `t = min(m, n−1)` (order-aware; one multiply per surviving coefficient per derivative step)"},"1940":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-731","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"1941":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-732","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polyder_cost`"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-733","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyint`"},"1943":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-734","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`m × n + m(m−1)/2` (order-aware; m passes each dividing n+j coefficients)"},"1944":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-735","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-736","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polyint_cost`"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-737","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`roots`"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-738","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`10n³`, `n = stripped companion dimension` (zero-leading/trailing coefficients stripped before companion matrix is built)"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-739","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED: delegates to `eigvals_cost` on trimmed degree"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-740","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:roots_cost`"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-741","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyfit`, `polyder`, and `polyint` return float64 regardless of the input dtype, so\ntheir real bill is `2×` the `flop_cost` above; `polyval` preserves the input dtype and\nbills at its own rate."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-742","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_polynomial.py`."},"1952":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-743","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Random ops are composite: the generation kernel cost and any setup cost\n(PRNG state update, rejection sampling) are folded into `flop_cost`; the\nweight tier **varies** by distribution family.  The full bill follows the\nfour-factor formula — draws bill their output dtype's rate (numpy's samplers\ndefault to float64, rate 2.0); complex dtypes are covered below."},"1953":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-744","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Weight tiers:"},"1954":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-745","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**weight 1.0** — uniform/integer/structural draws that are not module-level\nreorder ops: `rand`, `random`, `random_sample`, `ranf`, `uniform`, `randint`,\n`integers`, module-level `random.choice`, `multivariate_normal`."},"1955":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-746","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**weight 4.0** — reorder / resample ops, the same non-sequential access\ntier as sort and gather: module-level `random.sample`\n(an alias of `random_sample`'s draw, priced independently — see Sort and\nselect for the sibling sort/set family), module-level\n`random.permutation` and `random.shuffle`, and every `Generator`/`RandomState`\n`.choice` / `.permutation` / `.permuted` / `.shuffle` method. A Fisher-Yates\nreorder and a rejection-sampled choice both touch memory non-sequentially, the\nsame reason a sort does."},"1956":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-747","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**weight 16.0** — transcendental samplers (every continuous/transformed\ndistribution): `normal`, `standard_normal`, `randn`, `exponential`,\n`standard_exponential`, `poisson`, `binomial`, `geometric`,\n`hypergeometric`, `multivariate_hypergeometric`, `negative_binomial`,\n`multinomial`, `beta`, `dirichlet`, `f`, `gamma`, `gumbel`, `laplace`,\n`logistic`, `lognormal`, `logseries`, `pareto`, `power`, `rayleigh`,\n`standard_cauchy`, `standard_gamma`, `standard_t`, `triangular`,\n`vonmises`, `wald`, `weibull`, `zipf`, and all their Generator /\nRandomState counterparts."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-748","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Op / family"},"1958":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-749","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1959":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-750","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counti