gs":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"DECLARED: unpacks 8 bits per input byte; proportional to output"},"2405":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1196","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`_array_ops.py`"},"2406":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1197","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`mask_indices`"},"2407":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1198","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`max(numel(mask), n²)` (weight 1.0, priced at the mask's own dtype)"},"2408":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1199","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"DECLARED: scans the array `mask_func` produces, matching the `nonzero`/`flatnonzero`/`argwhere`/`count_nonzero` convention of billing `numel(input)`; floored at n² (the numel of the `ones((n, n), int)` probe `mask_func` receives as an argument, and can capture) so a `mask_func` that returns something smaller cannot buy a cheaper scan; the returned index arrays are not charged"},"2409":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1200","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`_array_ops.py`"},"2410":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1201","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`getitem` (`arr[key]`)"},"2411":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1202","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"basic indexing (int/slice/newaxis/Ellipsis, or a tuple thereof): `0` (view); advanced (fancy/boolean) indexing: `4·numel(output)` + `numel(mask)` per boolean-mask part (weight 1.0)"},"2412":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1203","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"DECLARED: fancy gather billed at the `take` rate; boolean-mask parts additionally scan like `compress`"},"2413":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1204","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`_ndarray.py`"},"2414":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1205","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`getitem` is the one op in this table with no module-level `fnp.<name>` call form — it\nbills `FlopscopeArray.__getitem__`, i.e. `arr[key]` syntax, not a function call. See\nthe unifying philosophy for\nthe basic-vs-advanced-indexing distinction this formula rests on."},"2415":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1206","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"`mask_indices` prices only the mask that `mask_func` produces, not the index arrays it\nreturns, so — unlike the dtype-neutral index generators below —\nits charge scales with the mask's dtype. The charge is also floored at n² (int dtype):\n`mask_func` receives numpy's internal `ones((n, n), int)` probe as an argument, so it\ncan capture that reference and return an arbitrarily small result while still having\nhad the full probe handed to it. `triu_indices`/`tril_indices` are separate ops with\ntheir own dtype-neutral formula and are unaffected by this."},"2416":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1207","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"These ops return coordinate/index arrays rather than values, and price\n**dtype-neutrally** — the billed count is `numel` of the returned index array(s)\nregardless of the array's actual integer dtype, since the work (computing positions)\ndoes not depend on how wide the position values happen to be stored:"},"2417":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1208","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"Op"},"2418":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1209","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2419":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1210","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"basis"},"2420":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1211","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`unravel_index`"},"2421":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1212","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel` of the returned index arrays"},"2422":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1213","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2423":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1214","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`ravel_multi_index`"},"2424":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1215","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel(output)` (= `N`, the number of index tuples)"},"2425":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1216","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2426":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1217","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`diag_indices`, `diag_indices_from`"},"2427":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1218","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel` of the returned index arrays"},"2428":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1219","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2429":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1220","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`tril_indices`, `tril_indices_from`"},"2430":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1221","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel` of the returned index arrays"},"2431":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1222","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2432":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1223","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`triu_indices`, `triu_indices_from`"},"2433":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1224","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel` of the returned index arrays"},"2434":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1225","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2435":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1226","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`tri`"},"2436":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1227","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel(output)` — **not** dtype-neutral; bills the actual output dtype like a value array (see Generator)"},"2437":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1228","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2438":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1229","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`broadcast_shapes`"},"2439":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1230","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"sum of `len(shape)` across the input shapes (floor 1)"},"2440":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1231","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2441":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1232","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`indices`"},"2442":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1233","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"`numel(output)` at the actual output dtype (dense: `len(dims)·prod(dims)`; sparse: `sum(dims)`)"},"2443":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1234","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"DECLARED"},"2444":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1235","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"All weight 1.0. Source: `src/flopscope/_array_ops.py`."},"2445":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1236","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"A free (weight-0) classification covers only an op's **pure data-movement / structural**\nbehavior. Any parameter, mode, or path that **computes or inspects values** is **charged**\nwith a reliable cost reusing the convention for that work; a path we cannot reliably bill\nis **rejected with a clear error**. These three ops carry weight **1.0** with a path-aware\n`flop_cost`:"},"2446":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1237","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"Op"},"2447":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1238","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"free path (`flop_cost = 0`)"},"2448":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1239","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"charged / rejected path"},"2449":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1240","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"`pad`"},"2450":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1241","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"— (no free path — every mode writes a fresh output buffer)"},"2451":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1242","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"`numel(output)` base for **every** mode; movement modes (`constant`, `edge`, `empty`, `wrap`, `reflect`/`symmetric` with `reflect_type='even'`) add nothing further on top of the base; `linear_ramp` and `reflect_type='odd'` add `(numel_out − numel_in)` on top of the base; stat modes `maximum`/`minimum`/`mean`/`median` reduce **every** axis — not only the ones actually padded — over a cross-section that **grows** as earlier axes get padded, adding `Σᵢ stats_i·stat_len_i·cross_i&#x60; on top of the base; **`mode=<callable>` raises**"},"2452":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1243","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"`trim_zeros`"},"2453":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1244","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"—"},"2454":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1245","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"`numel(input)` (value scan for the nonzero boundary)"},"2455":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1246","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"`copyto`"},"2456":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1247","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"—"},"2457":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1248","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"`numel(dst)` (or popcount(`where`) when masked) — every write is priced, same-dtype or not"},"2458":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1249","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"For `pad` stat modes: numpy pads axes in order `0..ndim-1`, mutating one shared output\nbuffer in place, so axis `i`'s reduction reads a cross-section built from axes `< i` at\ntheir *already-padded* size and axes `> i` at their original size:\n`cross_i = ∏_{j<i} grown_j · ∏_{j>i} in_shape[j]`, where `grown_j = in_shape[j] +\nbefore_j + after_j` is axis `j`'s final padded length. `stat_len_i = min(stat_length_i,\nin_shape[i])` (default = full axis). This reduction happens on **every** axis,\nincluding one with pad width `(0, 0)` — its result is simply discarded into a width-0\noutput region, but the FLOPs are real; only a wholly **empty** input (`numel_in == 0`,\nany axis length 0) skips the reduction loop entirely, mirroring numpy's own early-out.\nA full-axis stat (`stat_len` covering the whole axis on both sides) serves both sides\nfrom one reduction; otherwise numpy computes each side separately, even when one side's\nwidth is 0. `mean` adds one divide per stat output cell."},"2459":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1250","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"Worked example (`a = arange(5, dtype=int32)`, `pad_width=2` ⇒ `numel_out=9`,\n`numel_in=5`): `constant`, `edge`, `wrap`, `empty`, `reflect`, and `symmetric` each\nbill **9** (the `numel_out` base, no surcharge); `linear_ramp` and `reflect_type='odd'`\nbill **13** (`9 + (9−5)`); `maximum` bills **14** (`9 + 5`); `mean` bills **15**\n(`9 + (5 + 1 divide)`). This single-axis case can't show the cross-section growing, so\na second example: `a.shape == (3, 4)`, `pad_width=((1, 1), (0, 0))` (axis 0 padded on\nboth sides, axis 1 left completely unpadded) ⇒ `numel_out = 5·4 = 20`. `maximum` bills\n**52**: axis 0's reduction has `cross_0 = in_shape[1] = 4` and `stat_len_0 = 3`, costing\n`4·3 = 12`; axis 1's reduction — even though its pad width is `(0, 0)` — has\n`cross_1 = grown_0 = 5` (axis 0's *padded* length) and `stat_len_1 = 4`, costing\n`5·4 = 20`; total `20 + 12 + 20 = 52`. `mean` bills **61** (the same `12 + 20 = 32`\nreduction cost plus one divide per axis: `cross_0 + cross_1 = 4 + 5 = 9`;\n`20 + 32 + 9 = 61`)."},"2460":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1251","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"(`ravel_multi_index` — an index-math op with no free path — lives in the Index\ngenerators table, billed `numel(output)` and dtype-neutral.)"},"2461":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1252","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"**Complex dtypes**: `pad` bills factor 2 on every mode, including the movement modes —\nthey now write the same `numel(output)` base as every other mode. The charged\n`trim_zeros` bills factor 2 too (value scan / reduction). `copyto` resolves its billed\ndtype the general way — `np.result_type` over its source and destination operands —\nsee Which dtype prices a call."},"2462":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1253","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"Writing an array to disk is a real, billable side effect — even though it produces no\nnew in-memory array — while reading one back is not: the values already existed\nsomewhere (the caller's own prior compute paid for them, or they were supplied as\ninput data), so ingesting them is a free, view-like operation, the same treatment\n`from_dlpack` gets."},"2463":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1254","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"Op"},"2464":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1255","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2465":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1256","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"weight"},"2466":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1257","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"basis"},"2467":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1258","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`save`"},"2468":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1259","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`4 × (numel(array) + ndim(array) × 8)`"},"2469":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1260","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"1.0"},"2470":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1261","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"DECLARED: I/O write, priced per element serialized plus the array's shape header"},"2471":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1262","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`savez`, `savez_compressed`"},"2472":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1263","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`4 × (Σ numel(array_i) + Σ ndim(array_i) × 8 + Σ len(member name bytes) + 8)` (summed over every array passed, plus the byte length of a `__meta__` blob when present, plus the UTF-8 byte length of every archive member name including `\"__meta__\"`, plus one more 8-byte shape header for the names blob itself, present whenever the archive has at least one non-empty member name)"},"2473":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1264","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"1.0"},"2474":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1265","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"DECLARED: same per-element I/O price as `save`, one archive"},"2475":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1266","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`load`"},"2476":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1267","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`0`"},"2477":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1268","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"—"},"2478":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1269","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"DECLARED free: ingesting previously-computed values is not new compute"},"2479":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1270","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`from_dlpack`"},"2480":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1271","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`0`"},"2481":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1272","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"—"},"2482":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1273","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"DECLARED free: zero-copy ingest from another array library"},"2483":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1274","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"The `4×` constant is a flat write-amplification price or format overhead, not a literal\nbyte count — it multiplies with the normal `dtype_rate`/`complex_factor`/`weight` factors\nlike any other `flop_cost`, so a `save` of a float64 array bills `2×` the same call on\nfloat32 (`4 × numel × 2.0 × 1.0`), not a fixed byte size. `load` and `from_dlpack` have\nno cost path in code and are unconditionally free; `save`/`savez`/`savez_compressed`\nare the charged member of this family — writing an array to disk is metered, reading\none back is not."},"2484":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1275","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"Each array's `.npy`/`.npz` header also encodes its shape as one 8-byte integer per\ndimension — a channel a participant fully controls independent of the element count\n(`zeros((0, K))` has 0 elements but an arbitrary `K`, so a bare `4 × numel` price floors\nat a near-zero cost no matter how large `K` is). Every billed array's shape header\n(`ndim(array) × 8` bytes) is billed alongside its element data. `savez`/`savez_compressed`\nbill one further 8-byte shape header for the archive's member-names blob itself: the\nconcatenated member names are ingested server-side as one synthetic 1-D `uint8` array, so\nthey carry a shape header of their own, billed in-process too so the two paths match."},"2485":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1276","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"The optional `__meta__` dict `savez`/`savez_compressed` accept is JSON-serialized and\nwritten to the archive as a `uint8` byte array, exactly like any named array — so it\nbills the same per-byte egress cost (`4 × len(json-encoded blob)`, at the `uint8` dtype\nrate) and counts toward the `Σ numel(array_i)` sum above. It is not a free side channel:\nbilling it separately from the named arrays would let a participant round-trip arbitrary\ndata through `__meta__` at a flat, size-independent cost."},"2486":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1277","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"The archive's MEMBER NAMES — the `savez`/`savez_compressed` keyword-argument names,\nplus the auto-generated `arr_0`, `arr_1`, ... names for arrays passed positionally,\nplus the literal `\"__meta__\"` member when a meta block is present — are\nwritten into the archive and read back verbatim by `load`, exactly like array data, so\ntheir UTF-8 byte length is billed too, folded into the same total above. Without this, a\nparticipant could smuggle data through many tiny arrays given very large names (an\narchive member name can be tens of thousands of bytes) instead of through the array\nvalues, at a near-zero, name-independent cost."},"2487":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1278","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"`savez`/`savez_compressed` accept arrays positionally as well as by keyword, matching\n`numpy.savez`: a positional array is stored under its auto-generated `arr_N` name and\nbills exactly the same as the identical array passed under that name as a keyword (the\nmember-name byte cost above is identical either way). A keyword name that collides with\na positional array's auto-generated name raises the same error numpy raises, before any\nbilling happens."},"2488":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1279","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_io.py`."},"2489":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1280","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"Operations that apply a user-supplied callable across an array. flopscope bills the\nresult the wrapper materializes (numpy runs the callback itself)."},"2490":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1281","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"> **Submission caveat:** these run a Python callback *in-process* and raise\n> `RemoteCallbackError` on the client/server backend used for AIcrowd submissions, so\n> they cannot appear in submitted code — their cost matters only for local runs."},"2491":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1282","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"Op"},"2492":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1283","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2493":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1284","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"weight"},"2494":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1285","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"source"},"2495":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1286","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`apply_along_axis`"},"2496":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1287","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2497":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1288","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"1.0"},"2498":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1289","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`_counting_ops.py`"},"2499":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1290","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`apply_over_axes`"},"2500":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1291","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2501":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1292","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"1.0"},"2502":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1293","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`_counting_ops.py`"},"2503":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1294","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`fromfunction`"},"2504":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1295","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2505":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1296","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"1.0"},"2506":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1297","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`_array_ops.py`"},"2507":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1298","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`fromiter`"},"2508":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1299","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2509":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1300","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"1.0"},"2510":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1301","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`_array_ops.py`"},"2511":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1302","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`mask_indices`"},"2512":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1303","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`max(numel(mask), n²)`"},"2513":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1304","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"1.0"},"2514":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1305","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`_array_ops.py`"},"2515":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1306","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`piecewise`"},"2516":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1307","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`numel(input) × len(condlist)`"},"2517":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1308","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"1.0"},"2518":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1309","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`_counting_ops.py`"},"2519":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1310","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"`apply_along_axis` was price-cut from weight 4.0 to 1.0 (the wrapper's own\n`numel(output)` formula is unchanged; only the callback's *own* `fnp` calls, billed\nseparately, ever carried the higher tiers). `piecewise` moved from a flat\n`numel(input)` at weight 4.0 to `numel(input) × len(condlist)` at weight 1.0 — every\ncondition you pass rescans the input once, folded directly into `flop_cost` rather\nthan relying on the weight to do that work."},"2520":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1311","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"**Family rule**: operations that return a view, re-interpret memory, or leave a newly\nallocated buffer untouched (no per-element write) charge 0 FLOPs. See The unifying\nphilosophy and Views and\nmetadata for the full decision procedure and both\nrefinements — this entry is the family-table lookup, not a restatement of the rule."},"2521":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1312","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"Weight 0 now covers two sub-families:"},"2522":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1313","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"**Views / metadata** — no new buffer, or a read-only reinterpretation of an\nexisting one: `transpose`, `swapaxes`, `moveaxis`, `squeeze`,\n`expand_dims`, `flip`/`fliplr`/`flipud`, `rot90`, `atleast_1d`/`atleast_2d`/\n`atleast_3d`, `broadcast_to`, `asfortranarray`, `ascontiguousarray`,\n`view`, `real`/`imag` (component extraction — a strided view or constant-fill, no\narithmetic), `split`, `hsplit`, `vsplit`, `array_split`, `unstack`, `diagonal`\n(2-D view path only — the 1-D *construct* path writes, see Copy and\ngather), `linalg.diagonal`, `linalg.matrix_transpose`,\n`from_dlpack`, `load`, and all other shape/stride/dtype introspection (`ndim`,\n`shape`, `size`, `nbytes`, `itemsize`, `dtype`, `flags`, `base`, `data`, `ctypes`,\n`strides`, `T`, `isscalar`, `isfortran`). `astype`/`asarray` are **not** in this\nlist any more — they moved to the charged paragraph below; see Views and\nmetadata for their remaining narrow no-op\nexception."},"2523":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1314","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"**Untouched allocation** — a new buffer whose contents are the platform zero-page\ndefault, so nothing is actually written: `zeros`, `zeros_like`, `empty`,\n`empty_like`."},"2524":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1315","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"**Everything else that used to live in this family now writes real memory and is\ncharged.** `reshape`/`ravel`/`copy`/`flatten`/`require`, every materializing-copy op\n(`concatenate`/`stack`/`tile`/…), `astype`/`asarray` (any real cast or copy — see\nViews and metadata for the narrow\n`copy=False`/no-conversion no-op that stays free), and `fft.fftshift`/`fft.ifftshift`\nare weight 1.0; `take`/`take_along_axis`/`choose` (gather) are weight 4.0;\n`put`/`putmask`/`place`/`compress`/`extract`/`select`/3-arg `where`/`fill_diagonal`\n(scatter/select) are weight 1.0 (`where` gathers, so it is weight 4.0) — see Copy and\ngather for the full breakdown. `ones`/`full`/`eye`/`identity`/\n`tri`/`meshgrid` moved the same way — see\nGenerator for the constant-fill split that\nkeeps only `zeros`/`empty` (and their `_like` forms) free."},"2525":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1316","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_array_ops.py`."},"2526":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1317","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"The complete, per-op cost data lives in &#x2A;*`website/public/ops.json`** — one record per\noperation with `name`, `module`, `area`, `category`, `weight`, `cost_formula`,\n`cost_formula_latex`, `notes`, and `summary`. It is **generated** from the registry +\nweight tables by `scripts/generate_api_docs.py` and powers the website's API pages."},"2527":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1318","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"**Find an op:** filter `ops.json` by `name`, or browse the website API pages."},"2528":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1319","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"**Filter a family:** by `area` (`core` / `fft` / `linalg` / `random` / `stats`) or `module`."},"2529":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1320","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"**It can't drift:** CI runs `scripts/generate_api_docs.py --check`, which regenerates\n`ops.json` to a temp dir and fails if the committed file's **cost-model fields**\ndiffer (`weight`, `cost_formula`, `category`, `notes`, …). The `summary` field is\nsourced from the installed numpy's docstrings and is allowed to vary across the\nnumpy-version matrix, so it is excluded from the check — which means the gate also\nproves the cost model is numpy-version-independent. Every billed op is present\n(aliases resolve transitively to their canonical), enforced by\n`tests/test_cost_model_coverage.py`."},"2530":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1321","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"> **Granularity note.** `ops.json` is exhaustive in *coverage* — every op, with its\n> weight and a formula string — but its `cost_formula` is **coarse for many composite\n> `counted_custom` ops**, recording `per-operation` / `varies` where the real cost is\n> shape-dependent. For those, the closed form and its derivation live in the family\n> tables above. Treat `ops.json` as the complete index and this document as the precise\n> reference; the completeness test ties them together."},"2531":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"page","content":"How Flopscope Works","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Understanding Flopscope"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works"},"2532":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-0","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"The wrapping pattern"},"2533":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-1","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Cost interception"},"2534":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-2","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"Budget enforcement"},"2535":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-3","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-flow-of-a-single-call","content":"The flow of a single call"},"2536":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-4","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"The operation registry"},"2537":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-5","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#related-pages","content":"Related pages"},"2538":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-6","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","content":"*Understand how Flopscope wraps NumPy to count every FLOP.*"},"2539":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-7","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","content":"**You will learn:**"},"2540":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-8","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","content":"The wrapping pattern that makes `import flopscope.numpy as fnp` the counted NumPy surface"},"2541":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-9","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","content":"How costs are calculated from tensor shapes before execution"},"2542":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-10","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","content":"How budgets are enforced and what happens when they are exceeded"},"2543":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-11","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","content":"How the operation registry classifies every NumPy callable"},"2544":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-12","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"flopscope exposes a NumPy-compatible API. When you write `import flopscope.numpy as fnp` and call `fnp.einsum(...)`, you get a function that behaves like `np.einsum(...)` but with FLOP counting layered on top."},"2545":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-13","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"Under the hood, flopscope re-exports wrapped versions of NumPy functions. The `flopscope/__init__.py` module imports from internal modules that each handle a category of operations:"},"2546":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-14","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"**`_pointwise.py`** -- unary and binary elementwise operations (`exp`, `add`, `multiply`, etc.)"},"2547":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-15","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"**`_einsum.py`** -- the `einsum` and `einsum_path` functions with symmetry-aware path optimization"},"2548":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-16","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"**`_array_ops.py`** -- zero-cost operations (`zeros`, `reshape`, `transpose`, `copy`, etc.)"},"2549":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-17","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"**`_counting_ops.py`** -- operations that look free but involve genuine computation (`trace`, `histogram`, etc.)"},"2550":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-18","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"**`_sorting_ops.py`** -- sorting, searching, and set operations"},"2551":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-19","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"**Submodules** -- `flopscope.numpy.linalg`, `flopscope.numpy.fft`, `flopscope.numpy.random`, `flopscope.stats`"},"2552":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-20","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-wrapping-pattern","content":"Each wrapped function follows the same pattern: compute the analytical FLOP cost, check the budget, then delegate to the real NumPy implementation."},"2553":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-21","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"When you call a counted operation, flopscope computes its FLOP cost analytically from the tensor shapes *before* the operation executes. The cost depends on the operation category:"},"2554":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-22","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Category"},"2555":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-23","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Cost formula"},"2556":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-24","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Example"},"2557":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-25","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Pointwise unary"},"2558":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-26","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2559":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-27","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`fnp.exp(x)` on shape (256, 256) costs 65,536"},"2560":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-28","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Pointwise binary"},"2561":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-29","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2562":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-30","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`fnp.add(a, b)` with broadcast output (256, 256) costs 65,536"},"2563":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-31","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Reduction"},"2564":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-32","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`numel(input)`"},"2565":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-33","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`fnp.sum(x)` on shape (256, 256) costs 65,536"},"2566":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-34","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Einsum"},"2567":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-35","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"product of all index dimensions"},"2568":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-36","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`'ij,jk->ik'` with shapes (m, k), (k, n) costs m \\* k \\* n"},"2569":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-37","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Free"},"2570":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-38","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"0"},"2571":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-39","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"`fnp.zeros(...)`, `fnp.reshape(...)`, `fnp.transpose(...)`"},"2572":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-40","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"The cost is always deterministic -- the same shapes produce the same FLOP count regardless of the data values or the hardware running the code."},"2573":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-41","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#cost-interception","content":"Each FMA (fused multiply-add) counts as 2 operations (one multiply + one add). A matrix multiply of dimensions (m, k) x (k, n) costs `2*m*k*n − m*n` FLOPs."},"2574":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-42","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"`BudgetContext` accumulates the cost of every operation that runs inside it. Before each counted operation executes, the budget is checked:"},"2575":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-43","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"The wrapped function computes the analytical cost from input shapes"},"2576":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-44","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"It calls `budget.deduct(op_name, flop_cost=cost, ...)` on the active budget"},"2577":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-45","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"`deduct()` checks if `flops_used + cost > flop_budget`"},"2578":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-46","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"If within budget: the cost is recorded, and the real NumPy function runs"},"2579":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-47","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"If over budget: `BudgetExhaustedError` is raised, and the operation does **not** execute"},"2580":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-48","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"Every deduction is recorded as an `OpRecord` in the budget's operation log, capturing the operation name, input shapes, FLOP cost, cumulative total, context start offset, backend duration, and flopscope overhead duration. This log powers the budget summary and debugging tools."},"2581":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-49","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#budget-enforcement","content":"If no explicit `BudgetContext` is active, Flopscope automatically creates a global default context with a budget of 1e15 FLOPs (configurable via the `FLOPSCOPE_DEFAULT_BUDGET` environment variable). This means bare calls outside any `with` block still work and still count FLOPs."},"2582":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-50","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-flow-of-a-single-call","content":"Here is what happens when you call `fnp.matmul(A, B)` with shapes (100, 200) and (200, 50):"},"2583":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-51","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"The registry (`flopscope/_registry.py`) is a mapping of every NumPy callable to its classification and cost behavior. Each entry specifies:"},"2584":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-52","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"**Category:** one of `counted_unary`, `counted_binary`, `counted_reduction`, `counted_custom`, `free`, or `blacklisted`"},"2585":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-53","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"**Module:** which NumPy module it belongs to (`numpy`, `numpy.linalg`, `numpy.fft`, etc.)"},"2586":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-54","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"**Notes:** any special behavior or cost formula details"},"2587":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-55","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"The categories determine how costs are calculated:"},"2588":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-56","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Category"},"2589":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-57","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Meaning"},"2590":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-58","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Cost"},"2591":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-59","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`counted_unary`"},"2592":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-60","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Scalar math on each element"},"2593":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-61","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2594":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-62","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`counted_binary`"},"2595":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-63","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Element-wise binary operation"},"2596":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-64","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2597":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-65","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`counted_reduction`"},"2598":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-66","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Reduce an array along axes"},"2599":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-67","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`numel(input)`"},"2600":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-68","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"`counted_custom`"},"2601":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-69","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Bespoke cost formula"},"2602":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-70","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Varies (e.g., `n * ceil(log2(n))` for sort)"},"2603":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-71","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":