-1","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"Einsum: one event per orbit of (multiplications, accumulations)"},"2684":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-2","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"Reductions: `(unique inputs − unique outputs) + extra ops`"},"2685":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-3","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Inspecting what gets charged"},"2686":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-4","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"When numbers might surprise you"},"2687":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-5","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#whats-actually-happening","content":"What's actually happening"},"2688":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-6","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"*How flopscope reduces the FLOPs charged for `einsum`, `sum`, `mean`, `median`, and friends when your tensors have symmetric structure.*"},"2689":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-7","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"**You will learn:**"},"2690":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-8","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"The one mental model flopscope uses for all symmetry-aware FLOP savings"},"2691":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-9","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"How that model applies to `einsum`"},"2692":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-10","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"How it applies to reductions (`sum`, `mean`, `median`, …)"},"2693":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-11","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"How to inspect what gets charged with `flops.einsum_accumulation_cost` and `flops.reduction_accumulation_cost`"},"2694":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-12","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"When the savings don't materialize, and why"},"2695":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-13","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#the-mental-model","content":"When you declare symmetry on a tensor (with `flops.as_symmetric`) — or when flopscope detects it from a repeated operand object — some entries in the dense layout are mathematically interchangeable. An **orbit** groups together all entries that are interchangeable. flopscope charges one event per orbit, not per dense element."},"2696":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-14","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#the-mental-model","content":"For an `(n, n)` matrix declared symmetric in axes `(0, 1)`, there are `n*(n+1)/2` orbits among the `n*n` entries (the off-diagonal pairs share an orbit; diagonal entries are singleton orbits). Any cost that scales with \"number of distinct entries\" drops accordingly."},"2697":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-15","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"For an einsum, flopscope's charge in plain terms is:"},"2698":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-16","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"where `k` is the operand count. The savings come from *output orbits* — distinct output cells that flopscope can prove are interchangeable. The output gets symmetric structure either from declared symmetry on operands or from a repeated operand object appearing in multiple positions."},"2699":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-17","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"Worked example — triple outer product `'i,j,k->ijk'` of three rank-1 vectors of length 4:"},"2700":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-18","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"When you pass three distinct vectors, every (i, j, k) output cell is its own orbit — 192 events. When you pass the *same* vector in all three operand positions, the operand swap induces an `S₃` action on the output labels, collapsing the 64 dense output cells into 20 orbits — 60 events. The output is structurally symmetric and the cost reflects that."},"2701":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-19","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"The path that opt\\_einsum picks is irrelevant — the charge is **path-independent**. For multi-component expressions, each connected component is handled independently. Participants don't need to know which internal classifier fires for any given expression; only that the result is exact."},"2702":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-20","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"For reductions (`sum`, `prod`, `max`, `min`, `mean`, `median`, …), the formula is:"},"2703":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-21","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"The `− unique output cells` term is the off-by-one fix from #56: the first input element of each output cell is a free copy; only the remaining accumulations cost. So `sum` on `(10,)` charges 9 flops, not 10:"},"2704":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-22","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"With declared symmetry, both terms drop. Summing a fully-symmetric rank-3 tensor:"},"2705":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-23","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"`mean` adds one divide per unique output cell on top of the sum cost. `median` / `percentile` / `quantile` use a separate selection-style charge — see `flops.tier2_reduction_cost(...)`."},"2706":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-24","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Two public inspection functions return the same `AccumulationCost` shape:"},"2707":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-25","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Both are LRU-cached; warm-call latency is in microseconds. See `flops.einsum_cache_info()` and `flops.reduction_cache_info()` for cache state."},"2708":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-26","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"The orbit savings come from the **output** having symmetric structure. Declared symmetry on an input doesn't automatically translate to savings — the question is always whether the *output* cells share orbits."},"2709":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-27","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"**Gotcha 1 — input symmetry that doesn't reach the output.** Declaring `A` symmetric in `'ij,j->i'` doesn't save anything: the output index `i` doesn't share an orbit with anything under the (i, j) swap, so every output cell stays distinct."},"2710":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-28","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"**Gotcha 2 — reduction along an axis that breaks the symmetry.** Summing along axis 0 of a `(6, 6)` `S₂` matrix leaves a `(6,)` output. The `(0, 1)` swap doesn't fix axis 0, so the output has no residual symmetry and no savings appear:"},"2711":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-29","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"If your symmetry savings aren't materializing, check whether the operation's *output* would still carry symmetric structure given how the indices flow."},"2712":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-30","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#whats-actually-happening","content":"For curious contributor-level readers: the model is the α/M direct-event count, where α counts orbits of input entries projected onto the output and M counts orbits of inputs. The orbit calculation uses the *pointwise symmetry group* — the combination of declared symmetry on operands and the symmetries induced by repeated operand objects. The full classifier ladder (which picks between closed-form formulas like Young tableaux, singleton-cycle counts, and typed partition enumeration) lives in `flopscope/_accumulation/_ladder.py` and `flopscope/_accumulation/_regimes.py`."}},"count":2712},"sorting":{"language":"english","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{"content":{"docs":{"1":1265,"2":1291,"3":1819,"4":1368,"5":1832,"6":2234,"7":2050,"8":1946,"9":1947,"10":1948,"11":1775,"12":1420,"13":2279,"14":240,"15":213,"16":1928,"17":2448,"18":1910,"19":2517,"20":118,"21":1277,"22":1172,"23":940,"24":1478,"25":2299,"26":1852,"27":2580,"28":2040,"29":2054,"30":2385,"31":2039,"32":2055,"33":1434,"34":2175,"35":2025,"36":2354,"37":381,"38":2026,"39":1862,"40":1296,"41":2056,"42":1582,"43":1978,"44":1760,"45":1958,"46":1761,"47":196,"48":1274,"49":1955,"50":358,"51":357,"52":360,"53":359,"54":356,"55":355,"56":1971,"57":861,"58":25